window.RECORD_CITATIONS = {
 "fn-4": "<a href=\"https://www.japanesewiki.com/history/Jokamachi.html\"><code>jokamachi-wiki-corpus</code></a> - 「Jokamachi (castle towns) were cities that were established with the lord's castle in the center.」",
 "fn-5": "<a href=\"https://www.japanesewiki.com/history/Jokamachi.html\"><code>jokamachi-wiki-corpus</code></a> - 「The town distribution centered on the castle and had town districts for Samurai, Ashigaru (common foot soldier), townsmen, and temples.」",
 "fn-6": "<a href=\"https://www.japanesewiki.com/history/Jokamachi.html\"><code>jokamachi-wiki-corpus</code></a> - 「Samurai towns were where the large estates of retainers, the so called Buke yashiki (samurai residence), were located and in general, retainers with higher position had a closer location to the castle. These are reflected as current place names such as Sange, Kamiyashiki-cho, and Shimoyashiki-cho.」",
 "fn-7": "<a href=\"https://www.japanesewiki.com/history/Jokamachi.html\"><code>jokamachi-wiki-corpus</code></a> - 「The townsmen district was located outside of the Samurai towns and was the town for merchants and craftsmen. People from surrounding villages were moved into the joka and merchants and craftsmen were separated into their occupation. Current place names such as Gofuku-machi, Aburaya-cho, Daiku-cho, Kaji-machi, and Koya-machi are related to this.」",
 "fn-8": "<a href=\"https://www.japanesewiki.com/history/Jokamachi.html\"><code>jokamachi-wiki-corpus</code></a> - 「The Teramachi was located at the outer rim of the jokamachi and spacious temples formed part of the city defenses.」",
 "fn-9": "<a href=\"https://www.japanesewiki.com/history/Jokamachi.html\"><code>jokamachi-wiki-corpus</code></a> - 「However, regardless of disadvantages/advantages geographically, main route roads did not go through the back of the castle but passed through the Ote side or front side of the castle to indicate the glory of the ruler.」",
 "fn-10": "<a href=\"https://www.japanesewiki.com/history/Jokamachi.html\"><code>jokamachi-wiki-corpus</code></a> - 「Therefore, construction of Sogamae or surrounding the entire town with a moat and mounds increased and gradually the trend towards wall cities was seen. Major examples are Odawara-jo Castle of the Gohojo clan and Hideyoshi TOYOTOMI's Osaka-jo Castle.」",
 "fn-11": "<a href=\"https://okayama-castle.jp/learn-history-en/\"><code>okayama-castle</code></a> - 「His son, Hideie, determined to place Honmaru (main enclosure and keep) on the hill of Okayama and built Okayama Castle that stands to the present day. ... In addition, the branching channels of the Asahi River were devised as they are today to protect the north and east sides of the castle and utilized to create moats, and a castle town was built that stretched long in the north-south direction between the moats.」",
 "fn-13": "<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hirosaki_Castle\"><code>hirosaki-castle</code></a> - 「It was the seat of the Tsugaru clan, a 47,000 koku tozama daimyō clan which ruled over Hirosaki Domain, Mutsu Province」",
 "fn-14": "<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Himeji_Castle\"><code>himeji-castle</code></a> - 「The castle complex has a circumference of 4,200 m (2.6 mi).[5] It covers an area of 233 hectares (2,330,000 m2 or 576 acres)」",
 "fn-15": "<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Himeji_Castle\"><code>himeji-castle</code></a> - 「Area | 107 ha (260 acres)」 and 「The area within the middle moat of the castle complex is a designated Special Historic Site.」",
 "fn-16": "<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Himeji_Castle\"><code>himeji-castle</code></a> - 「From east to west, the Himeji Castle complex has a length of 950 to 1,600 m (3,120 to 5,250 ft), and from north to south, it has a length of 900 to 1,700 m (3,000 to 5,600 ft).」",
 "fn-17": "<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Himeji_Castle\"><code>himeji-castle</code></a> - 「The moats have an average width of 20 m (66 ft), a maximum width of 34.5 m (113 ft), and a depth of about 2.7 m (8.9 ft).」",
 "fn-18": "<a href=\"https://www.nakasendoway.com/the-story-of-hikone-castle-town/\"><code>hikone-castle-town</code></a> - 「In contrast, it is known from a census conducted in 1695 that there were 15,371 townspeople in Hikone, living in 53 separate wards.」 and, for the samurai being outside the count: 「All military personnel, whether samurai or ashigaru, were normally exempt from periodic census counts of population. It is difficult to know their precise numbers therefore, although one estimate for the end of the 17th century suggests there were 20,000 warriors in Hikone at that time.」",
 "fn-19": "<a href=\"https://www.nakasendoway.com/the-story-of-hikone-castle-town/\"><code>hikone-castle-town</code></a> - 「In contrast, it is known from a census conducted in 1695 that there were 15,371 townspeople in Hikone, living in 53 separate wards.」 and 「All military personnel, whether samurai or ashigaru, were normally exempt from periodic census counts of population. It is difficult to know their precise numbers therefore, although one estimate for the end of the 17th century suggests there were 20,000 warriors in Hikone at that time.」",
 "fn-20": "<a href=\"https://www.nakasendoway.com/the-story-of-hikone-castle-town/\"><code>hikone-castle-town</code></a> - 「15,371 townspeople in Hikone, living in 53 separate wards」",
 "fn-21": "<a href=\"https://edoflourishing.blogspot.com/2013/10/machi-towns-and-villages.html\"><code>edo-machi-kido</code></a> - 「Each machi 町 square was closed by a kido 木戸 wooden gate. The gates were open from 4 in the morning till about 10 in the evening.」 (the source's own English)",
 "fn-22": "<a href=\"https://www.atlantis-press.com/proceedings/ssha-23/125988752\"><code>qing-zhalan</code></a> - 「Dashilan is one of the most important commercial blocks in Beijing. Its was named from the luxurious fence gate at the entrance of Hutong. It's a public facility that cooperated with the curfew policy and block governance in ancient Beijing, especially in Ming Dynasty and Qing Dynasty.」",
 "fn-23": "<a href=\"https://www.nakasendoway.com/castle-towns-2/\"><code>jokamachi-zoning</code></a> - 「A typical pattern was for chonin to reside in wards forming narrow strips separating different groups of samurai. Usually such strips would be alongside the major thoroughfares, where access to the services they offered was most convenient for all people.」",
 "fn-24": "<a href=\"https://ihcsacafe-en.ihcsa.or.jp/news/josui/\"><code>edo-josui</code></a> - 「the 43-km waterway from the intake weir in Hamura to Yotsuya-Okido in Edo was completed on November 15, 1653.」",
 "fn-25": "<a href=\"https://www.library.metro.tokyo.lg.jp/portals/0/edo/tokyo_library/modal/index.html?d=5655\"><code>kanda-kakehi</code></a> - 「A kakehi (flume) runs above the Kanda River, with the Kanda Jōsui flowing inside it. The bridge seen in the distance between the bridge girders is Suidōbashi, and this view was one of the famous sights of Edo.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「神田川の上を懸樋が通り、中を神田上水が流れています。橋桁の間から見える遠景の橋が水道橋で、この景観は江戸の名所のひとつとなっていました。」)",
 "fn-26": "<a href=\"https://ihcsacafe-en.ihcsa.or.jp/news/josui/\"><code>edo-josui</code></a> - 「From Yotsuya-Okido pipes were buried underground to deliver water to the city. ... Since procurement was easy, wood was used for the waterpipes, which were called mokuhi.」",
 "fn-27": "<a href=\"https://ihcsacafe-en.ihcsa.or.jp/news/josui/\"><code>edo-josui</code></a> - 「Fine technology was required to ensure no leakage of water, and this was where ship carpenters and underground-storage carpenters displayed their skills.」",
 "fn-28": "<a href=\"https://www.jpx.co.jp/dojima/en/index.html\"><code>osaka-kurayashiki</code></a> - 「Feudal domains sold tax rice stored at kurayashiki (combined warehouses and residences) around Nakanoshima to rice brokers through auctions and issued rice bills (notes promising to exchange for rice) to successful bidders. The rice bills, including those to exchange for rice to be delivered to Osaka, were actively traded.」",
 "fn-30": "<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rice_broker\"><code>asakusa-kuramae</code></a> - 「The rice brokers in Edo were called fudasashi (札差, \"note/bill exchange\"), and were located in the kuramae (蔵前, \"before the storehouses\") section of Asakusa.」 (the source's own English)",
 "fn-31": "<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rice_broker\"><code>asakusa-kuramae</code></a> - 「Merchants in Osaka and Edo thus began to organize storehouses where they would store a daimyō's rice in exchange for a fee, trading it for either coin or a form of receipt; essentially a precursor to paper money. Many if not all of these rice brokers also made loans, and would become quite wealthy and powerful.」 and 「A very profitable business, fudasashi acted both as usurers and as middlemen organizing the logistics of daimyō tax payments to the shogunate.」",
 "fn-32": "<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rice_broker\"><code>asakusa-kuramae</code></a> - 「The rice brokers, like other elements of the chōnin (townspeople) society in Edo, were frequent patrons of the kabuki theatre, Yoshiwara pleasure district, and other aspects of the urban culture of the time.」",
 "fn-33": "<a href=\"https://www.library-archives.pref.fukui.lg.jp/bunsho/category/tenji/32442.html\"><code>fukui-bushi-jutaku</code></a> - 「\"Suganuma go-ke ezu\" (Plan of the Suganuma House), 1839 ... This is the mansion plan of the Suganuma family, holders of a 1,000-koku fief, near the Yanagi gate (today, in the vicinity of the courthouse). The site was about 1,000 tsubo, 28 ken (about 59 m) north-south and 32.5 ken (about 51 m) east-west. The north side faces the street, and there is a front gate (omote-gomon) there.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「「菅沼御家絵図」1839年 ... 柳御門近く（現、裁判所付近）の知行千石の菅沼家の屋敷図です。南北28間（約59 ｍ）、東西32.5間（約51 m）の約千坪の敷地がありました。北側が通りに面し、表御門があります。」) and 「The Suganuma family were of yoriai-seki rank ... from the Jōkyō halving of stipends (1686) onward, they held 1,000 koku.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「菅沼家は寄合席格で ... 貞享の半知（1686年）以降、1千石となりました。」) (\"'Suganuma house plan', 1839 ... the plot plan of the Suganuma family, 1,000 koku, near the Yanagi gate: about 1,000 tsubo, 28 ken north-south, 32.5 ken east-west, facing the street on the north with its front gate.\" / \"The Suganuma were of yoriai rank ... 1,000 koku from the 1686 halving.\")",
 "fn-34": "<a href=\"https://online.bunka.go.jp/heritages/detail/144338\"><code>shibata-ashigaru-nagaya</code></a> - 「A nagaya in which the low-ranking samurai of the former Shibata domain lived, eight dwellings joined together in one building. One dwelling is an entrance hall, two six-mat rooms, a three-mat room and a kitchen - quite cramped, and plain in feel.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「旧新発田藩の下級武士の住んでいた長屋で八軒を一棟に連らねている。一軒分は玄関に六畳二室、三畳、台所でかなりせまく、素朴な感じがする。」)",
 "fn-35": "<a href=\"https://online.bunka.go.jp/heritages/detail/144338\"><code>shibata-ashigaru-nagaya</code></a> - 「Length along the ridge 43.6 m, depth between the beams 7.3 m, hipped roof (yosemune-zukuri), thatched, with an eave on the east face, shingled (kokera-buki).」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「桁行43.6m、梁間7.3m、寄棟造、茅葺、東面庇付、こけら葺」)",
 "fn-36": "<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rice_broker\"><code>asakusa-kuramae</code></a> - 「A very profitable business, fudasashi acted both as usurers and as middlemen organizing the logistics of daimyō tax payments to the shogunate.」",
 "fn-37": "<a href=\"https://news.cgtn.com/news/3d3d674d7755444d33457a6333566d54/index.html\"><code>shaoxing-towpath</code></a> - 「The towpath in Shaoxing takes two forms: one is on the bank of the canal as most people would expect, the other kind is in the middle of the water course parallel to the bank.」 and 「These towpath bridges are mostly slab stones placed on top of countless stone piers, each about 0.5 meters above the water.」",
 "fn-40": "<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Imperial_City,_Beijing\"><code>beijing-imperial-city</code></a> - 「Further to the south was the \"Corridor of a Thousand Steps\" outside Chengtianmen Gate, to either side of which are the offices of the various government ministries.[citation needed]」",
 "fn-41": "<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nagoya_Castle\"><code>nagoya-castle</code></a> - 「Besides serving as the residence of the lord, the palace functioned as the administrative center of the feudal government.」 (of the Ninomaru Palace) and 「Its core spaces, such as the Ninomaru Palace, functioned as both the lord's residence and the supreme government office for domain administration.」",
 "fn-42": "<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nagoya_Castle\"><code>nagoya-castle</code></a> - 「Surrounding this core, the San-no-maru enclosure accommodated high-ranking senior retainers and elder statesmen (karō).」<!-- a second passage naming the Sannomaru Oyakata auxiliary residences was quoted here until 2026-09-12 and is not on the page -->",
 "fn-43": "<a href=\"https://www.matsumoto-castle.jp/eng/about/palace\"><code>matsumoto-goten</code></a> - 「However, because the Ninomaru was small in size, only about two thirds of the size of the Honmaru Palace, the county office and the town office was transferred to Rokku town, and the conference room for daimyo and village headmen was transferred to Agetsuchi town.」",
 "fn-44": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/藩校\"><code>hanko-jawiki</code></a> - 「A hankō is a school that the various domains founded in the Edo period in order to educate the children of their retainers.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「藩校（はんこう）は、江戸時代に、諸藩が藩士の子弟を教育するために設立した学校。」) and 「Hankō were founded in the domain's own territory at the domain's expense, but ...」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「藩校は、藩の費用負担により藩地に設立されたが」)",
 "fn-45": "<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/City_Wall_of_Nanjing\"><code>nanjing-wall-enwiki</code></a> - 「The difference was that it adopted a winding, free style, based on the city's complicated topography.」 and 「The construction concentrated on military defenses because the city was at the foot of a mountain—a natural barrier to control the commanding elevation with the river as its natural city moat.」",
 "fn-46": "<a href=\"https://zh.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E4%B8%8A%E6%B5%B7%E8%80%81%E5%9F%8E%E5%8E%A2\"><code>shanghai-xiancheng-zhwiki</code></a> - 「In 1553, in order to resist the wokou pirates coming upriver, a city wall ringing the town was built for the first time.」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「1553年为抵御溯江而上的倭寇第一次修建了环城的城墙。」) and 「On this English-language map of Shanghai published in 1933, the circular outline of the old walled town (laochengxiang) is still clearly visible.」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「在这幅1933年出版的英文上海地图上，老城厢的圆形轮廓仍清晰可见」)",
 "fn-47": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E8%97%A9%E5%BA%81\"><code>hancho-jawiki</code></a> - 「The hanchō of an Edo-period han was the daimyo's residence castle or dwelling; set in the castle or in a mansion, it carried out the administrative business of the territory the daimyo ruled. Han of castle-holding daimyo rank and above had it in the castle; han of castle-lord grade and below - the castle-lord grade and the castle-less daimyo - were not permitted to maintain a castle, and so placed a mansion in their territory and made it the hanchō.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「江戸時代の藩の藩庁は、大名の居城や居所のことで、城や屋敷に設置して大名が支配する領内の行政業務を執り行う。城持大名以上の藩は城に、城主格以下の城主格と無城大名の藩は城を構えることが許されなかったことから、領内に屋敷を置いて藩庁とした。」)",
 "fn-48": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E6%9C%AC%E4%B8%B8\"><code>honmaru-jawiki</code></a> - 「The honmaru of the early modern period held the oku-goten, the inner palace where the castle lord and his family lived, and the omote-goten, the outer palace that was the seat of government, with a multi-storied keep and turrets built up alongside them as required ...」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「近世における本丸は、城主とその家族が暮らす奥御殿と政庁である表御殿を置き、必要に応じて重層な天守や櫓を建て並べ」)",
 "fn-49": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E6%8E%9B%E5%B7%9D%E5%9F%8E\"><code>kakegawa-castle-jawiki</code></a> - 「Yamauchi Kazutoyo, who had been a direct vassal of Toyotomi Hideyoshi, entered Kakegawa castle at 51,000 koku (later 59,000 koku).」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「掛川城には豊臣秀吉の直臣であった山内一豊が5万1千石（のち5万9千石）で入った。」) and 「At this time the Ninomaru goten, which was the administrative office, the drum turret, the Fuki gate and the Ōte second gate were rebuilt by 1861 (Bunkyū 1), but ...」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「この際、政務所である二ノ丸御殿、太鼓櫓、蕗の門、大手二の門は1861年（文久元年）までに再建されたが」)",
 "fn-50": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E5%AE%B6%E8%80%81\"><code>karo-jawiki</code></a> - 「As for important matters, they assembled at the hyōjōsho or the like on fixed days and settled them after joint deliberation.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「重要事項については定例日に評定所などに集合して合議の上で決裁する。」) and 「There were also cases in which a karō held a lower mansion (shimo-yashiki) separate from his own residence; basically they kept their mansions near the castle, which was a defensive facility.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「家老が自分の屋敷とは別に下屋敷を有する場合もあり、基本的に防衛施設である城の近くに屋敷を構えていた。」) (\"Important matters were decided by assembling at the hyōjōsho or the like on fixed days and deliberating in council.\" / \"A karō sometimes had a lower mansion besides his own; basically they kept their mansions near the castle, a defensive facility.\")",
 "fn-51": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/陣屋\"><code>jinya-jawiki</code></a> - 「Moreover, in the jin'ya of a small domain one sees an arrangement in which, as a facility ranking next to a castle, earthen ramparts and a moat are run around the perimeter while no high stone walls are provided, and the residence, the offices, the retainers' official quarters, storehouses, a drill ground and the like are laid out within.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「また、小藩の陣屋では、城に準ずる施設として周囲に土塁や堀をめぐらせつつも高い石垣は備えず、居館・役所・家臣の役宅や土蔵、調練場などを配置する構成がみられる」)",
 "fn-53": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/藩校\"><code>hanko-jawiki</code></a> - 「As a nationwide tendency, the martial arts too were encouraged at the hankō: pupils entered at seven or eight, first learned letters, later studied the martial arts, and graduated from fourteen or fifteen to about twenty.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「全国的な傾向として、藩校では武芸も奨励され、7歳・8歳で入学して第一に文を習い、後に武芸を学び、14歳・15歳から20歳くらいで卒業する。」) and 「In their period of expansion they came to 255 schools nationwide, founded in almost every domain.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「発展期には全国に255校に上り[3]、ほぼ全藩に設立された。」) (\"As a nationwide tendency the hankō also encouraged the martial arts: pupils entered at 7 or 8, learned letters first, then the martial arts, graduating between about 14-15 and 20.\" / \"At their height they reached 255 schools nationwide, founded in nearly every domain.\")",
 "fn-54": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E6%97%A5%E6%96%B0%E9%A4%A8\"><code>nisshinkan-jawiki</code></a> - 「This facility serves at once as a museum, a dōjō (an archery hall and a martial-arts hall), a training and lodging facility, a film studio location and so on ...」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「この施設は、博物館・道場（弓道場・武道場）・研修や宿泊施設・映画撮影所などを兼ねており」) and 「Until the age of fifteen they belonged to the sodokusho, the recitation hall (the elementary school), and studied etiquette, calligraphy, martial arts and the like.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「15歳までは素読所（小学）に属し、礼法、書学、武術などを学んだ。」)",
 "fn-55": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E6%9C%B1%E9%9B%80%E5%A4%A7%E8%B7%AF\"><code>suzaku-oji-jawiki</code></a> - 「Heijō-kyō's Suzaku Ōji was 73 meters wide, and in Heian-kyō it came to be 82 meters wide (Engishiki).」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「平城京の朱雀大路は幅73メートル、平安京では幅82メートル（延喜式）[6]となった。」), 「There is also an estimate putting Heian-kyō's Suzaku Ōji at 84.0 meters in total width, with a roadway width of 70.2 meters inside the side ditches.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「平安京朱雀大路は全幅84.0メートル、側溝より内側の道路幅70.2メートルとする推定もある[9]。」) and 「Because the Suzaku Boulevard (Zhuque Dajie) of Tang Chang'an was as much as 147 meters wide ...」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「唐・長安の朱雀大街は幅147メートルもあったため」) (\"Heijō-kyō's Suzaku avenue was 73 m wide; Heian-kyō's 82 m (Engishiki).\" / \"There is also an estimate of 84.0 m overall for Heian-kyō's Suzaku avenue, 70.2 m of roadway inside the side ditches.\" / \"Tang Chang'an's Suzaku street was as much as 147 m wide.\")",
 "fn-56": "<a href=\"https://www.library-archives.pref.fukui.lg.jp/bunsho/category/tenji/32442.html\"><code>fukui-bushi-jutaku</code></a> - 「There was a site of about 1,000 tsubo, 28 ken (about 59 m) north-south and 32.5 ken (about 51 m) east-west.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「南北28間（約59 ｍ）、東西32.5間（約51 m）の約千坪の敷地がありました。」)",
 "fn-57": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E6%97%A5%E6%96%B0%E9%A4%A8\"><code>nisshinkan-jawiki</code></a> - 「The Nisshinkan's school buildings were completed immediately west of Aizu-Wakamatsu castle. On a site about 120 ken east-west and about 60 ken north-south, furnished with a swimming ground said to be the oldest pool in Japan and even with an observatory, it was one of the foremost hankō in the country.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「会津若松城の西隣に日新館の校舎が完成。東西約120間、南北およそ60間の敷地に日本最古のプールといわれる水練場や天文台までをも備えた、全国有数の藩校であった。」)",
 "fn-58": "<a href=\"https://ihcsacafe-en.ihcsa.or.jp/news/josui/\"><code>edo-josui</code></a> - 「In particular, one of the dams there, called the Nagewatashi Weir, consisted of a combination of cedar logs, fascines (bundles of tree branches), and gravel that could be easily removed.」 and 「the 43-km waterway from the intake weir in Hamura to Yotsuya-Okido in Edo」",
 "fn-59": "<a href=\"https://ihcsacafe-en.ihcsa.or.jp/news/josui/\"><code>edo-josui</code></a> - 「At the beginning of the seventeenth century it built the Kanda Josui waterway, which had its source in the present-day Inokashira Pond in Inokashira Park, Kichijoji.」",
 "fn-60": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E7%8E%89%E5%B7%9D%E4%B8%8A%E6%B0%B4\"><code>tamagawa-josui-jawiki</code></a> - 「The roughly 43 kilometers from Hamura City to the Ōkido are all open-cut ...」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「羽村市から大木戸までの約43キロメートルは全て露天掘りで」)",
 "fn-61": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E7%8E%89%E5%B7%9D%E4%B8%8A%E6%B0%B4\"><code>tamagawa-josui-jawiki</code></a> - 「It also bore the role of discharging surplus water into the Shibuya River (the Onden River) ...」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「余った水を渋谷川（穏田川）へと放流する役割も担っていた」)",
 "fn-62": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E6%90%A6%E6%89%8B%E9%96%80\"><code>karamete-jawiki</code></a> - 「The karamete-mon is one of the castle gates: the gate of the karamete entrance at the rear, opened opposite the ōte-mon.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「搦手門（からめてもん）は城門の一つで、大手門に対して開かれる裏手にあたる搦手口の門。」)",
 "fn-63": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E6%B1%9F%E6%88%B8%E5%9F%8E%E4%B8%89%E5%8D%81%E5%85%AD%E8%A6%8B%E9%99%84\"><code>edo-36-mitsuke-jawiki</code></a> - 「The \"thirty-six mitsuke of Edo castle\" is a listing of the 36 principal ones among the mitsuke (watch guardhouses) placed at Edo castle. Many were installed as a single unit with a bridge thrown across the moat.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「江戸城三十六見附（えどじょうさんじゅうろくみつけ）とは、江戸城に置かれた見附（見張り番所）のうちの主な36か所を挙げたもの。堀に架けられた橋と一体的に設置されたものが多い。」) and 「In fact there seem to have been many more watch stations at Edo's castle gates (various accounts give 66, 90 and so on), but according to the records of the bakufu's works office there were 26 gates with masugata gatehouses in the outer enceinte ... and for the sake of how it sounded, ten notable gates were added to the 26 masugata gates to make the \"thirty-six mitsuke\" ...」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「実際には、江戸の城門の見張り場所自体はもっと多数あったようだが（66、90など諸説あり）、枡形門を持つ見附は、幕府作事方の資料によると外曲輪に26門あり ... 語呂の関係から、枡形の26門に、目ぼしい10門を足して「三十六見附」とし」) (many were built as one with a bridge over the moat.\" / \"In fact there seem to have been many more watch-posts (66, 90 by various accounts); by the bakufu works office's records there were 26 masugata gates in the outer works ... for the sake of the ring of it, 10 notable gates were added to the 26 to make 'thirty-six'.\")",
 "fn-64": "<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Imperial_City,_Beijing\"><code>beijing-imperial-city</code></a> - 「Further to the south was the \"Corridor of a Thousand Steps\" outside Chengtianmen Gate, to either side of which are the offices of the various government ministries.」",
 "fn-65": "no publicly readable source (searched 2026-09-12: the count of nine boat-entry canals at Edo was searched for and no readable page gives it. The pages reached carry the Nagoya canal instead - it is quoted at the note below - and one of them puts that canal's modern length at 16.2 km against the 6 km of the original cut, which is a different figure for a different thing)",
 "fn-66": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E5%A0%80%E5%B7%9D_(%E5%90%8D%E5%8F%A4%E5%B1%8B%E5%B8%82)\"><code>horikawa-nagoya-jawiki</code></a> - 「Its origin is held to lie in its having been cut, on the occasion of the founding of Nagoya in the early Edo period, as a canal for carrying building materials from Ise Bay to the vicinity of Nagoya castle.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「江戸時代初期の名古屋開府に際して、建築資材運搬用の運河として伊勢湾から名古屋城付近まで開削されたことがそのルーツとされる。」) and 「It is often said to have been dug in Keichō 15 (1610) by Fukushima Masanori on the orders of Tokugawa Ieyasu, as a waterway intended for carrying materials, on the occasion of the tenka-bushin (the shogunate-ordered construction levy on the domains) for the building of Nagoya castle ...」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「慶長15年（1610年）、福島正則が徳川家康の命によって掘削したとされることが多い。名古屋城築城の天下普請に際した、資材運搬を目的とした水路として」)",
 "fn-67": "<a href=\"https://zh.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E9%98%8A%E9%97%A8\"><code>changmen-zhwiki</code></a> - 「The more important reason Changmen enjoys its great fame is still that in the Ming and Qing periods this area was once the most flourishing commercial district in all of China.」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「阊门享有盛名更重要的原因，还是因为明清时期这一带曾经是全中国最繁盛的商业街区。」) and 「It included Nanhao Street (today's Nanhao Street), Shangtang Street and Shantang Street, which radiate outward outside the wall, as well as Changmen Avenue inside the wall (today's Xizhongshi). ... At that time the shops of every kind here numbered as many as tens of thousands, with every trade and calling represented.」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「包括城外呈放射状的南濠街（今南浩街）、上塘街和山塘街，以及城内的阊门大街（今西中市）。... 当时这里各种店铺多达数万家，各行各业应有尽有」)",
 "fn-68": "<a href=\"https://zdic.net/hans/%E5%85%B3%E5%8E%A2\"><code>guanxiang-zdic</code></a> - 「guanxiang ... the streets outside a city gate and the area nearby.」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「关厢 ... 城门外的街道及附近区域。」) / 「English: suburb outside city gate」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「英文 suburb outside city gate」)",
 "fn-69": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E9%8A%AD%E6%B9%AF\"><code>sento-jawiki</code></a> - 「It is said that in the Bunka era of the early nineteenth century (1804-1818) there were more than 600 sentō in the whole of Edo, whose population was about 1,000,000 to 1,200,000.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「19世紀初期の文化年間（1804〜1818年）には、人口約100万〜120万人の江戸全体で600軒以上の銭湯があったという。」)",
 "fn-70": "<a href=\"https://kotobank.jp/word/%E8%B3%AA%E5%B1%8B-73884\"><code>shichiya-kotobank</code></a> - 「The pawnbroker of the Edo period was a short-term lender on movable property, supplementing the lives of the common population as the money economy and the culture of technique, centered on the three cities (Osaka, Kyoto, Edo), spread out into the provinces. In eighteenth-century Edo there were 2,700 pawnshops, and in Osaka in the middle of the nineteenth century 2,400.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「江戸時代の質屋は，三都（大坂，京都，江戸）を中心とする貨幣経済と技術文化の浸透が地方に展開するなかで，民衆生活を補完する短期動産金融業者である。18世紀江戸では2700軒，19世紀中ごろの大坂では2400軒の質屋が存在した。」) and, from the second published encyclopedia on the same page, 「From the figures of 2,731 in the Edo pawnbrokers' guild in 1723 (Kyoho 8), 628 Kyoto pawnshops in 1699 (Genroku 12), and 2,420 Osaka pawnshops at the end of the shogunate, one can imagine the place the pawnshop held in early-modern popular finance.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「1723年（享保8）の江戸質屋仲間2731、1699年（元禄12）の京都質屋628、幕末の大坂質屋2420という数字からも、近世庶民金融に占める質屋の地位は想像できよう。」) (two published encyclopedias reprinted on one page, giving the count independently. The per-head ratio is not on the page: 2,731 shops against a million inhabitants is one per about 370.)",
 "fn-71": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E6%B1%9F%E6%88%B8%E4%B8%89%E5%BA%A7\"><code>edo-sanza-jawiki</code></a> - 「The Edo sanza were the playhouses permitted to stage kabuki by the Edo town magistrate's office from the middle through the later Edo period. ... At first Edo had a great many playhouses, but they were gradually consolidated to four houses, and finally became three.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「江戸三座（えど さんざ）は、江戸時代中期から後期にかけて江戸町奉行所によって歌舞伎興行を許された芝居小屋。... 江戸には当初数多くの芝居小屋があったが、次第に整理されて四座になり、最終的に三座となった。」) and 「The Edo sanza were the playhouses licensed by the Edo town magistracy for kabuki from the mid to late Edo period ... Edo at first had many playhouses, which were gradually reduced to four and finally three.\" / \"In 1714 the Yamamura-za was abolished, leaving the three - Nakamura-za, Ichimura-za and Morita-za.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「正徳4年（1714年）には山村座が取り潰されて中村座・市村座・森田座の江戸三座となる」)",
 "fn-72": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/小塚原刑場\"><code>kozukappara-jawiki</code></a> - 「In the Edo period there were the Kozukappara execution ground to the north and, to the south, the Suzugamori execution ground along the Tōkaidō (Minami-Ōi, Shinagawa ward, Tokyo), and they were called the two great execution grounds. The size of the ground was about 60 ken (108 meters) of frontage and a little over 30 ken (54 meters) in depth.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「江戸時代は北に小塚原刑場、南に東海道沿いの鈴ヶ森刑場（東京都品川区南大井）があり、二大刑場といわれた。刑場の広さは間口60間（108メートル）、奥行30間余（54メートル）程だった。」) (the size sentence carries no citation on the article)",
 "fn-73": "<a href=\"https://zh.wikipedia.org/wiki/西安城墙\"><code>xian-wall-zhwiki</code></a> - 「The wall is nearly rectangular in plan, about 13.74 kilometers in circumference, enclosing an area of about 12 square kilometers.」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「城墙平面近长方形，周长约13.74公里，围合区域约12平方公里。」)",
 "fn-74": "<a href=\"https://zh.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E8%A5%BF%E5%AE%89%E5%9F%8E%E5%A2%99\"><code>xian-moat-zhwiki</code></a> - 「On the outer side of the wall there is a moat, 20 meters wide and 10 meters deep, holding water all year round, which together with the wall forms a complete defensive system.」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「城墙的外侧有护城河，宽20米，深10米，终年积水，和城墙共同构成完整的防御体系。」)",
 "fn-75": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E7%8E%89%E5%B7%9D%E4%B8%8A%E6%B0%B4\"><code>tamagawa-josui-jawiki</code></a> - 「Water was drawn from the Tama River at the Hamura intake weir, flowed east across the Musashino plateau, and passed through the \"water-watch station\" (mizubansho) attached to the Yotsuya Okido, which stood near the present-day Yotsuya 4-chome intersection [the stored quote breaks off here]. The full sentence continues: \"... and was distributed into the city of Edo. The roughly 43 kilometers from Hamura city to the Okido are all open-cut, and below the water-watch station it is an underground conduit using wooden pipes and stone pipes.\"」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「羽村取水堰で多摩川から取水し、武蔵野台地を東流し、現在の四谷四丁目交差点付近にあった四谷大木戸に付設された「水番所」（水番屋）を経て、江戸市中へと分配されていた。羽村市から大木戸までの約43キロメートルは全て露天掘りで、水番所以下は木樋や石樋を用いた地下水道である。」) (below the water station it is an underground conduit of wooden and stone pipes.\")",
 "fn-77": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E8%8F%A9%E6%8F%90%E5%AF%BA\"><code>bodaiji-jawiki</code></a> - 「A bodaiji (菩提寺) is a temple whose sect a household has been devoted to for generations and at which it holds memorial services for the enlightenment (bodai) of its ancestors.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「菩提寺（ぼだいじ）とは、一家が代々その寺の宗旨に帰依して、先祖の菩提を弔う寺院のこと[1]。」)",
 "fn-78": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E6%9D%B1%E6%B5%B7%E9%81%93\"><code>tokaido-jawiki</code></a> - 「In the 2nd month of Keicho 9, Okubo Nagayasu and others were ordered to make the width of the highway 5 ken, to plant hackberry (enoki) trees along the roadside, to fix 1 ri as 36 cho, to set a ri marker at every ri, and to fix the pack-horse charges at each post station.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「慶長9年2月に大久保長安その他に命じて街道の幅員を5間とし、路傍に榎樹を植え、1里＝36町と決め、1里ごとに里堠を設け、各駅の駄賃を定めた。」)",
 "fn-79": "<a href=\"https://ihcsacafe-en.ihcsa.or.jp/news/josui/\"><code>edo-josui</code></a> - 「Hamura ... was 126 meters above sea level, and the difference in altitude to Yotsuya-Okido, the destination, was just 92 meters. So a drop of only 21 cm could be made every 100 meters.」 and 「A total length of 42.74 kilometers was built within the 92.3 meter difference in elevation from Hamura to Yotsuya.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「羽村から四谷までの高低差92.3メートルの間に全長42.74キロメートルが築かれた」)",
 "fn-80": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E7%8E%89%E5%B7%9D%E4%B8%8A%E6%B0%B4\"><code>tamagawa-josui-jawiki</code></a> - 「The main line from Hamura to the Yotsuya Okido is laid along a chosen ridge line of the Musashino plateau, and besides that the large distributary channels too are laid, for the most part, along chosen watersheds between the rivers within the Musashino plateau.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「羽村から四谷大木戸までの本線は武蔵野台地の尾根筋を選んで引かれているほか、大規模な分水路もおおむね武蔵野台地内の河川の分水嶺を選んで引かれている」)",
 "fn-81": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E7%8E%89%E5%B7%9D%E4%B8%8A%E6%B0%B4\"><code>tamagawa-josui-jawiki</code></a> - 「At the site of the old Yotsuya Okido, the terminus of the Tamagawa Josui ... in former times there was a water-watch station here, and from this point onward the water was distributed to every part of the city of Edo through buried stone pipes and wooden pipes.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「玉川上水の終点である旧四谷大木戸地点には ... 往時、ここに水番所があり、ここから先は埋設された石樋・木樋を通して江戸市中各地へと配水していた。」)",
 "fn-82": "<a href=\"https://ihcsacafe-en.ihcsa.or.jp/news/josui/\"><code>edo-josui</code></a> - 「Pipes went to daimyo and samurai residences and to the row houses where the townspeople lived in half of Edo's numerous districts. And wells were installed in the row houses, so people never had to go far to collect water.」",
 "fn-83": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E5%A4%A7%E5%9D%82%E5%9F%8E\"><code>osaka-castle-jawiki</code></a> - 「Below the plateau to the north the Yodo River and its tributaries flow, and while serving the function of a natural moat they were at the same time used to draw water into the moats inside the castle.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「北の台地下には淀川とその支流が流れており、天然の堀の機能を果たすとともに、城内の堀へと水を引き込むのに利用された。」)",
 "fn-84": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E5%8F%82%E9%81%93\"><code>sando-jawiki</code></a> - 「In the narrow sense it denotes only the passage inside the sacred bounds, such as within the torii or the temple gate; in the broad sense it means the whole of the road running to the shrine or temple from a place of heavy foot traffic such as a highway.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「狭義には、鳥居や山門などの結界内の通路のみを示すが、広義には街道筋など人通りの多いところから社寺に至る道の全てを意味する。」), 「Generally, there are also many places where the sando is recognized together with the torii-front town or gate-front town that arose of its own accord around an influential shrine or temple,」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「参道は、一般的に、影響力がある神社や寺の周辺で、自然発生した鳥居前町・門前町と合わせて認識されている場所も多く」) and 「Also, places where the line of a highway was bent so that it came close to a shrine or temple for the sake of pilgrimage were not rare either,」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「また、参詣のために街道筋が捻じ曲げられて社寺に近づいていたところなども、珍しくはなく」) (\"Narrowly it means only the path inside the sacred bounds of the torii or gate; broadly it means the whole way from a busy place such as a highway to the shrine or temple.\" / \"Sandō are often recognized together with the torii-front and gate-front towns that grew up around influential shrines and temples.\" / \"It was not rare for a highway to be bent toward a shrine or temple for the convenience of pilgrims.\")",
 "fn-85": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E8%8F%A9%E6%8F%90%E5%AF%BA\"><code>bodaiji-jawiki</code></a> - 「A bodaiji (菩提寺) is a temple whose sect a household has been devoted to for generations and at which it holds memorial services for the enlightenment (bodai) of its ancestors.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「菩提寺（ぼだいじ）とは、一家が代々その寺の宗旨に帰依して、先祖の菩提を弔う寺院のこと[1]。」) and 「For example, the Tokugawa house's Kan'eiji and Zojoji are generally called bodaiji, while the Date house's Zuihoji is called a kogein, and so on.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「例えば、徳川家の寛永寺や増上寺は一般に菩提寺と呼び、伊達家の瑞鳳寺は香華院と呼ぶなど。」)",
 "fn-86": "<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chang%27an\"><code>changan-enwiki</code></a> - 「There were about 110 separately walled wards, two markets, suburbs, villas for lower-level government officials, and religious institutions.」, 「The West and East Markets would open at noon, announced by the 300 strikes on a loud drum, while the markets would close one hour and three quarters before dusk, the curfew signaled by the sound of 300 beats to a loud gong.」 and 「This was the only holiday where the government lifted its nightly curfew all across the city so that people could freely exit their wards and stroll about the main city streets」",
 "fn-87": "<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Architecture_of_the_Song_dynasty\"><code>song-architecture-enwiki</code></a> - 「During the Tang dynasty (618–907), urban areas in China were strictly divided into distinct residential and commercial wards divided by city walls」 and 「Due to intensely growing commercialism during the Song period, shops could now line streets in residential areas and did not have to be situated behind precinct walls.」",
 "fn-88": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E8%94%B5%E5%89%8D\"><code>kuramae-jawiki</code></a> - 「The Asakusa Okura had eight channels cut on the right bank of the Sumida River, counted from upstream as number one, number two and so on, and many rice storehouses stood in a row facing them.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「浅草御蔵は、隅田川の右岸に上流から一番、二番と数える8本の堀を作り、それに面した多くの米蔵が連なった。」)",
 "fn-89": "<a href=\"https://zh.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E5%B9%B3%E6%B1%9F%E5%9B%BE\"><code>pingjiang-tu</code></a> - 「There are five city gates opened in all - Qi Gate, Lou Gate, Feng Gate, Pan Gate and Chang Gate - and each of these gates is drawn with a land gate and a water gate at the same time」 (translated from the Chinese (simplified) by this project; original: 「共开有齐门、娄门、葑门、盘门、阊门五座城门，每座城门均同时绘制了陆门及水门」)",
 "fn-90": "<a href=\"https://www.japanesewiki.com/history/Jokamachi.html\"><code>jokamachi-wiki-corpus</code></a> - 「only the lord's residence castle was surrounded by a moat and castle wall and the town area itself was not surrounded by a castle wall in the initial jokamachi. However, as jokamachi developed in Japan, they increased in economic and political value and the necessity for their safeguard against war arose. Therefore, construction of Sogamae or surrounding the entire town with a moat and mounds increased」",
 "fn-91": "<a href=\"https://www.rujiazg.com/article/11577\"><code>dongjing-menghualu-rujia</code></a> - 「In the Eastern Capital, every ward has a military patrol post at every three hundred paces, and on high ground there are fire-watch towers, with men above keeping watch and, quartered below, a hundred soldiers together with water buckets, sprinkling brooms, hooks and saws, axes and forks, ladders and ropes and the like; whenever a fire breaks out, they beat it out and it is put out in a moment.」 (translated from the Chinese (classical, Song) by this project; original: 「东京每坊三百步有军巡铺，又于高处有望火楼，上有人探望，下屯军百人及水桶、洒帚、钩锯、斧权、梯索之类，每遇生发，扑救须臾便灭。」)",
 "fn-92": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E7%81%AB%E8%91%AC%E5%A0%B4\"><code>kasoba-jawiki</code></a> - 「By around 1650, almost all the temples in Asakusa and Shitaya within the city of Edo had a crematory in their precincts. In Kanbun 7 (1667) ... the precinct crematories of the twenty-odd temples scattered through Asakusa and Shitaya were moved together to a site designated by the bakufu, established near the Kozukappara execution ground.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「1650年ごろには江戸市中の浅草や下谷のほとんどの寺院が境内に火葬場を有していた。寛文7年（1667年）には ... 浅草や下谷に散在していた20数ケ寺の境内火葬場を小塚原刑場近くに設けた幕府指定地へ集合移転させた。」)",
 "fn-93": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/藩校\"><code>hanko-jawiki</code></a> - 「In their period of expansion they came to 255 schools nationwide, founded in almost every domain.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「発展期には全国に255校に上り[3]、ほぼ全藩に設立された。」)",
 "fn-94": "<a href=\"https://zh.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E6%BC%8F%E6%BE%A4%E5%9C%92\"><code>louzeyuan-zhwiki</code></a> - 「On 3 February 1104 Cai Jing proposed setting up louzeyuan (漏澤園) so that unclaimed and destitute remains might be gathered and buried on government land; the louzeyuan system was formally established, and thereafter each successive dynasty carried it on unchanged, the Yuan and the Ming both establishing them」 (translated from the Chinese (traditional) by this project; original: 「蔡京於1104年2月3日建議設置漏澤園以官地收葬無主及窮乏骸骨，漏澤園制度正式確立，隨後各朝一直沿襲未改，元明皆有設立」)",
 "fn-95": "<a href=\"https://zh.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E6%BC%8F%E6%BE%A4%E5%9C%92\"><code>louzeyuan-zhwiki</code></a> - 「A louzeyuan was generally sited on high, open, barren ground, with a fence built all around to stop idlers and sundry persons from entering. Each person buried had a burial plot of 8 or 9 chi, 3 chi deep, numbered by the Thousand Character Classic, recording such particulars as the dead person's name, native place, and the year, month and day.」 (translated from the Chinese (traditional) by this project; original: 「漏澤園一般選在高曠不毛之地，四周建有圍欄，阻閑雜人等進入。埋葬者有葬地8或9尺，[7]，深3尺，以千字文為號，記死者姓名、鄉貫、年月日等資料。」)",
 "fn-96": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/鈴ヶ森刑場\"><code>suzugamori-jawiki</code></a> - 「In the Edo period it was an execution ground set up along the southern entrance to Edo (the Tokaido), together with the Kozukappara execution ground, which was set up along the northern entrance (the Nikko kaido).」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「江戸時代には、江戸の北の入口（日光街道）沿いに設置されていた小塚原刑場とともに、南の入口（東海道）沿いに設置されていた刑場であった。」)",
 "fn-97": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E7%81%AB%E8%91%AC%E5%A0%B4\"><code>kasoba-jawiki</code></a> - 「By around 1650, almost all the temples in Asakusa and Shitaya within the city of Edo had a crematory in their precincts. In Kanbun 7 (1667), when the fourth shogun Tokugawa Ietsuna went to Kan'eiji in Ueno to visit the graves, the foul smoke of cremation reaching him became a problem, and the precinct crematories of the twenty-odd temples scattered through Asakusa and Shitaya were moved together to a site designated by the bakufu, established near the Kozukappara execution ground. ... The Kozukappara crematory thereafter continued as the largest crematory in Edo for the 220 years until it ceased operating in Meiji 20 (1887).」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「1650年ごろには江戸市中の浅草や下谷のほとんどの寺院が境内に火葬場を有していた。寛文7年（1667年）には4代将軍徳川家綱が上野寛永寺へ墓参に赴いた際に、火葬の臭煙が及んだことが問題になって、浅草や下谷に散在していた20数ケ寺の境内火葬場を小塚原刑場近くに設けた幕府指定地へ集合移転させた。... 小塚原火葬場はその後明治20年（1887年）に操業停止するまでの220年間に亘り江戸最大の火葬場として存続する。」)",
 "fn-98": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/小塚原刑場\"><code>kozukappara-jawiki</code></a> - 「In Kanbun 7 (1667) Teiyo Gikan, the head priest of Eko-in in Honjo, founded a Jogyo hall on land adjoining the Kozukappara execution ground in order to bury the dead and hold memorial services for them. This later became Minami-Senju Eko-in.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「寛文7年（1667年）に本所回向院の住職である弟誉義観（ていよぎかん）が、死者の埋葬と供養を行うため、小塚原刑場隣接地に常行堂を創建した。これが後の南千住回向院になった。」) and 「In the Meiji period the track of the Joban line passed through the middle of the grounds and cut them in two; the north side of the Joban line became Eko-in and the south side became independent as Enmei-ji. The site of the execution ground ... lies within Enmei-ji.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「明治期に常磐線の線路が敷地中央を通過して分断され、常磐線の北側が回向院、南側が延命寺として独立した。刑場跡は ... 延命寺内に位置する。」) (\"In 1667 Teiyo Gikan, abbot of Honjo Ekō-in, founded a Jōgyō hall on ground adjoining the Kozukappara execution ground to bury and pray for the dead; this became Minami-Senju Ekō-in.\" / \"In the Meiji era the Jōban line cut the site in two, the north becoming Ekō-in and the south Enmei-ji; the execution-ground site lies within Enmei-ji.\")",
 "fn-99": "<a href=\"https://zh.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E6%BC%8F%E6%BE%A4%E5%9C%92\"><code>louzeyuan-zhwiki</code></a> - 「From the Northern Song onward, louzeyuan were without exception managed by Buddhist monks, and a house was also built inside the louzeyuan for the monk who kept the ground to live in. A louzeyuan was generally sited on high, open, barren ground, with a fence built all around」 (translated from the Chinese (traditional) by this project; original: 「自北宋以來，漏澤園皆由佛教僧人打理[5]，漏澤園中還建有屋舍，供守園僧居住[6]。漏澤園一般選在高曠不毛之地，四周建有圍欄」)",
 "fn-100": "<a href=\"https://www.japanesewiki.com/history/Jokamachi.html\"><code>jokamachi-wiki-corpus</code></a> - 「Keihaimono (low-ranked people) such as Ashigaru were placed on the outside of the townsmen district and current place names include Ban-cho, Yumino-cho, and Teppo-cho.」",
 "fn-101": "<a href=\"https://www.nakasendoway.com/the-story-of-hikone-castle-town/\"><code>hikone-castle-town</code></a> - 「Naotake was rewarded for his services by receiving the forfeited lands of his brother and additional lands besides. Valued now at 290,000 koku, Omi province had become one of the twenty most valuable possessions in Japan.」",
 "fn-102": "<a href=\"https://up.codes/s/open-burning-and-recreational-fires\"><code>ifc-307-bonfire</code></a> - 「307.4.1 ... A bonfire shall not be conducted within 50 feet (15 240 mm) of a structure or combustible material unless the fire is contained in a barbecue pit. Conditions which could cause a fire to spread within 50 feet (15 240 mm) of a structure shall be eliminated prior to ignition.」",
 "fn-103": "<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chang%27an\"><code>changan-enwiki</code></a> - 「The overall form of the city[when?] was an irregular rectangle. The ideal square of the city had been twisted into the form of the Big Dipper for astrological reasons, and also to follow the bank of the Wei River.」",
 "fn-104": "<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/City_Wall_of_Nanjing\"><code>nanjing-wall-enwiki</code></a> - 「Construction of the Beijing and the Xi'an city walls was in the ancient style of square or rectangular design.」",
 "fn-106": "<a href=\"https://zh.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E5%B9%B3%E6%B1%9F%E5%9B%BE\"><code>pingjiang-tu</code></a> - 「Its city plan takes the form of a rectangle, long north to south and short east to west; there are five city gates opened in all - Qi Gate, Lou Gate, Feng Gate, Pan Gate and Chang Gate - each of them drawn with a land gate and a water gate at the same time, and outside the city there is a broad moat.」 (translated from the Chinese (simplified) by this project; original: 「其城市平面呈南北长东西短的长方形，共开有齐门、娄门、葑门、盘门、阊门五座城门，每座城门均同时绘制了陆门及水门，城外有宽阔的护城河。」)",
 "fn-107": "<a href=\"https://kotobank.jp/word/%E5%BE%A1%E7%94%A8%E9%83%A8%E5%B1%8B-66403\"><code>goyobeya-kotobank</code></a> - 「The room inside Edo Castle where the tairo, roju and wakadoshiyori were in attendance and conducted the business of government.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「江戸城内で、大老・老中・若年寄が詰めて政務を執った部屋。」) and 「Note also that in the domain office (hancho) of each domain and in their Edo mansions as well, the room where the karo carried out their duties was called the goyobeya.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「なお各藩の藩庁や江戸屋敷でも家老が執務する部屋を御用部屋とよんだ。」)",
 "fn-108": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E8%A9%95%E5%AE%9A%E6%89%80\"><code>hyojosho-jawiki</code></a> - 「It stood at Tatsunokuchi outside Edo Castle (the present southwestern part of number 4, Marunouchi 1-chome, Chiyoda ward, the position where the Marunouchi Eiraku Building stands ...), and was the body that tried important matters of bakufu government, suits by daimyo and hatamoto, and problems spanning the jurisdictions of several magistrates; it was composed of the town magistrate, the temple-and-shrine magistrate, the finance magistrate, and one roju.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「江戸城外の辰ノ口（現在の千代田区丸の内一丁目4番南西部で丸の内永楽ビルディングがある位置。...）にあり、幕政の重要事項や大名・旗本の訴訟、複数の奉行の管轄にまたがる問題の裁判を行なった機関で、町奉行、寺社奉行、勘定奉行と老中1名で構成された。」)",
 "fn-111": "<a href=\"https://www.library.metro.tokyo.lg.jp/portals/0/edo/tokyo_library/modal/index.html?d=5655\"><code>kanda-kakehi</code></a> - 「A kakehi (flume) runs above the Kanda River, with the Kanda Jōsui flowing inside it. The bridge seen in the distance between the bridge girders is Suidōbashi, and this view was one of the famous sights of Edo.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「神田川の上を懸樋が通り、中を神田上水が流れています。橋桁の間から見える遠景の橋が水道橋で、この景観は江戸の名所のひとつとなっていました。」)",
 "fn-112": "<a href=\"https://zh.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E8%A5%BF%E5%AE%89%E5%9F%8E%E5%A2%99\"><code>xian-moat-zhwiki</code></a> - 「On the outer side of the wall there is a moat, 20 meters wide and 10 meters deep, holding water all year round, which together with the wall forms a complete defensive system.」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「城墙的外侧有护城河，宽20米，深10米，终年积水，和城墙共同构成完整的防御体系。」)",
 "fn-113": "<a href=\"https://edoflourishing.blogspot.com/2013/10/machi-towns-and-villages.html\"><code>edo-machi-kido</code></a> - 「Each machi 町 square was closed by a kido 木戸 wooden gate. The gates were open from 4 in the morning till about 10 in the evening.」 (the source's own English)",
 "fn-114": "<a href=\"https://www.atlantis-press.com/proceedings/ssha-23/125988752\"><code>qing-zhalan</code></a> - 「Dashilan is one of the most important commercial blocks in Beijing. Its was named from the luxurious fence gate at the entrance of Hutong. It's a public facility that cooperated with the curfew policy and block governance in ancient Beijing, especially in Ming Dynasty and Qing Dynasty.」",
 "fn-115": "<!-- SUMMARY-ONLY --><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.18874/jjrs.27.3-4.2000.297-178\"><code>bernstein-fire-and-earth</code></a> - 「In the summer of 1873, the Meiji government's Council of State declared a nationwide ban on cremation ... cremation supporters appropriated state-sanctioned values and aims to win repeal of the ban only two years after it went into effect.」 (abstract only); the May 1873 proposal and the stench read on a secondary account: 「In May 1873, they suggested relocating crematoriums, notably the Senju temple ones, outside the city's \"red line\" (shubiki, 朱引). They claimed the \"severe stench [from burning bodies] injures people's health.\"」 (the source's own English)",
 "fn-116": "no publicly readable source (searched 2026-09-14: \"Ming Qing China no resident hereditary military aristocracy gentry local elite versus Japanese samurai castle town comparison urban form\" and \"Qing magistrate residence inside yamen rule of avoidance rotating appointee three-year term\", with Columbia's Asia for Educators pages and the Wikipedia article on the scholar-official read; the nearest passage, that the examination bureaucracy completely replaced the aristocracy, supports the absence of a hereditary office-holding elite and not the comparison of the two cities' forms, which is this page's own; Sen-dou Chang's chapter on the morphology of walled capitals in Skinner's The City in Late Imperial China is the work that would settle it and is in print only)",
 "fn-117": "<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yamen\"><code>yamen-enwiki</code></a> - 「Typically, the bureaucrat and his immediate family would live in a residence attached to the yamen.」; <a href=\"https://afe.easia.columbia.edu/qing/state.html\"><code>columbia-qing-state</code></a> - 「Officials also served in each position for a relatively short period of time (usually three years) before rotating to a new position」; <a href=\"https://afe.easia.columbia.edu/qing/state.html\"><code>columbia-qing-state</code></a> - 「an official could not serve in his home province or even in a province adjacent to his own province」",
 "fn-118": "<a href=\"https://baike.baidu.com/en/item/Yangzhou%20City%20Site/934310\"><code>baidu-yangzhou-city-site</code></a> - 「The Luocheng was located below Shu Hill and was the commercial, handicraft, and residential area, expanded during the mid-Tang period.」; <a href=\"https://baike.baidu.com/en/item/Yangzhou%20City%20Site/934310\"><code>baidu-yangzhou-city-site</code></a> - 「The Zicheng was the area for government offices and yamen, built by utilizing the Sui Dynasty Jiangdu Palace City and the East City.」",
 "fn-119": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E6%9D%B5%E7%AF%89%E5%9F%8E\"><code>kitsuki-castle-jawiki</code></a> - 「It is a natural stronghold, enclosed by the Takayama River to the north and Morie Bay to the east.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「北は高山川、東は守江湾に囲まれた天然の要害である。」); <a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E6%9D%B5%E7%AF%89%E5%9F%8E\"><code>kitsuki-castle-jawiki</code></a> - 「It was built atop Daiyama, at the mouth of the Yasaka River.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「八坂川の河口にある台山（だいやま）の上に築かれた。」); <a href=\"https://www.kyu-shokyoto.org/kitsuki/jyouka/\"><code>kyu-shokyoto-kitsuki</code></a> - 「Centered on Kitsuki Castle, there are groups of samurai residences on the high ground to the north and south, with the merchants' town sandwiched in the valley between them.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「杵築城を中心に、南北の高台に武家屋敷群があり、その谷間に商人の町が挟まれています。」)",
 "fn-120": "<a href=\"https://zh.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E9%87%8C%E5%9D%8A%E5%88%B6\"><code>lifang-zhwiki</code></a> - 「The Song abolished the Tang's ward-and-market system and the night curfew, producing commercial streets, entertainment quarters and night markets.」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「宋代取消唐代的市坊制與夜禁規定，形成商業街、瓦肆與夜市。」); <a href=\"https://zh.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E9%87%8C%E5%9D%8A%E5%88%B6\"><code>lifang-zhwiki</code></a> - 「The ward system ceased to exist after the Song and Yuan.」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「里坊制度於宋、元之後不復存在。」); <a href=\"https://www.rujiazg.com/article/7581\"><code>wugou-lifang-rujia</code></a> - 「In the Song dynasty in which Lü Dafang and Zhu Xi lived, the ward system had already broken up and the ward walls had long since been torn down」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「在吕大防与朱熹生活的宋朝，里坊制已经瓦解，坊墙早已被推倒」)",
 "fn-121": "<a href=\"https://kotobank.jp/word/%E4%BF%B8%E7%A6%84%E5%88%B6-1414874\"><code>kotobank-horokusei</code></a> - 「The fief village is kept nominally, while the retainer receives, from the domain's granary, the tax rice the domain has collected at the domain's official tax rate」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「知行村を名目的には残しながら藩公定の年貢率で藩が収納した物成米を藩庫からうけとる」); <a href=\"https://kotobank.jp/word/%E4%BF%B8%E7%A6%84%E5%88%B6-1414874\"><code>kotobank-horokusei</code></a> - 「A system under which retainers were paid kuramai (stipend rice) by their lord」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「家臣が領主から蔵米(俸禄米)を給与された制度」)",
 "fn-122": "<a href=\"https://k.sina.cn/article_6582636570_1885b101a00100t9j4.html\"><code>sina-neixiang-xianya</code></a> - 「In front of the great hall are the six fang - Personnel, Revenue, Rites, War, Punishments and Works - running north to south, with Personnel, Revenue and Rites on the east side and War, Punishments and Works on the west.」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「大堂前为吏、户、礼、兵、刑、工六房，自北向南，东边为吏、户、礼房，西边为兵、刑、工房。」)",
 "fn-123": "<a href=\"https://k.sina.cn/article_7072274192_p1a58a571000100kdl2.html\"><code>sina-pingyao-xianya</code></a> - 「It occupies twenty-six thousand square meters, with over two hundred rooms」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「占地两万六千平方米，房屋二百余间」); <a href=\"https://www.travelchinaguide.com/attraction/shanxi/pingyao/ancient-government-office.htm\"><code>travelchinaguide-pingyao-yamen</code></a> - 「With a length of 203 meters (222 yards) and a width of 131 meters (143 yards), it covers approximately 26,000 square meters (31,095 square yards).」",
 "fn-124": "no publicly readable source (searched 2026-09-13: the pages read describe a 水番所/guardhouse regulating flow, not a settling tank)<!-- feature 238: converted from a bare inline marker in the prose, which read \"(unsourced: the pages read describe a 水番所/guardhouse regulating flow, not a settling tank)\". No claim changed and no source was sought; the label moved to the assertion, where feature 195 requires it and where the footnote census can count it. -->",
 "fn-125": "<a href=\"https://edokara.tokyo/conts/2020/06/12/1485\"><code>edokara-yosuibaki</code></a> - 「A facility called a yosuibaki, through which, in the Edo period, water was discharged in order to regulate the flow of the Tamagawa josui」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「江戸時代、玉川上水の水量調整のため、排水を流した、余水吐（よすいばき）という施設」); <a href=\"https://edokara.tokyo/conts/2020/06/12/1485\"><code>edokara-yosuibaki</code></a> - 「It is the valley south of Yotsuya Okido. Through this valley once ran the Shibuya River, whose source was the spring-fed pond inside Shinjuku Gyoen」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「四谷大木戸の南にある谷です。この谷にはかつて、新宿御苑の中にある湧水池を源流とする渋谷川が流れていました」) (the spillway is the page's; the aqueduct's surplus went to a valley and a river, and that a moat is where it would go is this page's inference)",
 "fn-126": "no publicly readable source (searched 2026-09-13: the cited sources give temple locations and counts, not a defensive reading, and one explicitly denies it)<!-- feature 238: converted from a bare inline marker in the prose, which read \"(unsourced: the cited sources give temple locations and counts, not a defensive reading, and one explicitly denies it)\". No claim changed and no source was sought; the label moved to the assertion, where feature 195 requires it and where the footnote census can count it. -->",
 "fn-127": "no publicly readable source (searched 2026-09-13: the ja.wikipedia 水道橋 URL on record is a disambiguation page with no etymology)<!-- feature 238: converted from a bare inline marker in the prose, which read \"(unsourced: the ja.wikipedia 水道橋 URL on record is a disambiguation page with no etymology)\". No claim changed and no source was sought; the label moved to the assertion, where feature 195 requires it and where the footnote census can count it. -->",
 "fn-128": "no publicly readable source (searched 2026-09-13: the page gives the wall's own circumference as 13.74 km and no moat length or feed)<!-- feature 238: converted from a bare inline marker in the prose, which read \"(unsourced: the page gives the wall's own circumference as 13.74 km and no moat length or feed)\". No claim changed and no source was sought; the label moved to the assertion, where feature 195 requires it and where the footnote census can count it. -->",
 "fn-129": "<a href=\"https://zh.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E5%8C%97%E4%BA%AC%E5%9F%8E%E6%B1%A0\"><code>beijing-chengchi-zhwiki</code></a> - 「The water of Beijing's moat system comes from the northwest - Yuquan Mountain, Baifu Spring and the like - reaching Gaoliang Bridge outside Xizhimen by way of the Chang He, then dividing into two routes ... finally emptying into the Tonghui River」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「北京护城河水系来水方向为西北玉泉山、白浮泉等地，经长河抵西直门外高梁桥，然后分为两路...最后注入通惠河」); <a href=\"https://zh.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E5%8C%97%E4%BA%AC%E5%9F%8E%E6%B1%A0\"><code>beijing-chengchi-zhwiki</code></a> - 「Outside the wall there is a moat 30-60 m wide.」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「城外有宽30-60米的护城河。」); <a href=\"https://zh.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E5%8C%97%E4%BA%AC%E5%9F%8E%E6%B1%A0\"><code>beijing-chengchi-zhwiki</code></a> - 「When the outer city wall was built in the Jiajing reign, a moat was dug as well.」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「嘉靖朝修筑外城墙时，也开凿了护城河。」) (the palace, inner and outer circuits each carried a moat; the imperial city's own is not stated on the page)",
 "fn-130": "<a href=\"https://crd.ndl.go.jp/reference/entry/index.php?id=1000279632&page=ref_view\"><code>ndl-crd-hikifune</code></a> - 「Going downstream was one thing, but working up against the current was crushingly heavy labor. ... River transport could not have existed without human-hauled towing.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「川の下りはともかくとして、流れに遡っての上りは大変な重労働だった。...人力による曳き船なしに河川の舟運は成り立たなかった。」); <a href=\"https://crd.ndl.go.jp/reference/entry/index.php?id=1000279632&page=ref_view\"><code>ndl-crd-hikifune</code></a> - 「Against the wind, towing was used - the river boat drawn along by a long rope from the embankment on the riverbank.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「逆風の場合は、河川沿岸の土手より長い綱で、川舟を曳いて航行する曳舟が行われた。」)",
 "fn-131": "<a href=\"https://www.thepaper.cn/newsDetail_forward_1420523\"><code>thepaper-dongjing-population</code></a> - 「By the fifth year of Tianxi (1021), because of the great accumulation of population outside the city, they were reorganized again into nine xiang.」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「到了天禧五年（1021年），又由于城市外部人口大量聚集，所以重新调整为9厢。」); <a href=\"https://www.thepaper.cn/newsDetail_forward_1420523\"><code>thepaper-dongjing-population</code></a> - 「Li Hequn, in his doctoral thesis A Study of the Layout of Northern Song Dongjing, notes that the nine xiang outside the wall held fourteen fang in all; the small number of fang is owing to the many imperial and private gardens in the outskirts. The population is therefore estimated at around 150,000-200,000.」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「李合群在其博士论文《北宋东京布局研究》中提到，城外9厢一共14坊，坊少的原因在于郊外大量皇家、私家园林所致。所以人口估算在15-20万左右。」) (the settlement outside Kaifeng's wall is the page's, in population and administrative wards; that it was commerce sprawling past ramparts that no longer mattered is this page's reading)",
 "fn-132": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E6%B5%84%E9%96%91%E5%AF%BA\"><code>jokanji-jawiki</code></a> - 「It comes from the fact that when large numbers of courtesans died in the Ansei earthquake (1855), they were buried at this temple as if thrown in」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「安政の大地震（1855年）で大量の遊女が死亡した際にこの寺に投げ込むように葬られたことによる」); <a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E6%B5%84%E9%96%91%E5%AF%BA\"><code>jokanji-jawiki</code></a> - 「It stands near the Yoshiwara pleasure quarter」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「吉原遊廓の近くにあり」) (the distance is this page's own measurement between the temple and the quarter)",
 "fn-133": "no publicly readable source (searched 2026-08-09: for the duty cycle of a moat sluice - whether it rested open, shut or throttled. No page read describes one. The throttled reading is reasoned from the through-flow this map declares and from what a wet moat loses to seepage, and it decides only that ONE glyph is drawn rather than an open and a shut one)<!-- feature 238: the roster called it a GUESS while the body set out the regime in detail -->",
 "fn-134": "no publicly readable source (searched 2026-08-09: for the lifting mechanism of a sluice board - the Japanese encyclopedia articles on the water gate and the sluice gate, and the reference entry for the latter, none of which describes the board-in-grooves frame or the windlass. It stands as reasoning, and it is what the glyph draws)<!-- feature 238: the roster said it stands as reasoning while the body described the mechanism as fact -->",
 "fn-135": "no publicly readable source (searched 2026-09-13: for Edo's surviving moats blooming green today; on no page read. The claim it decorates - that a quiet moat was not kept clean and was managed by occasional dredging and by the carp kept in it - is the part the map acts on)<!-- feature 238: the roster recorded this one as not found while the sentence asserted it -->",
 "fn-136": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E8%94%B5%E5%B1%8B%E6%95%B7\"><code>kurayashiki-jawiki</code></a> - 「Osaka is said to have had 80 domain kurayashiki in the Enpo era (1670s) and 125 in the Tenpo era (1840s)」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「大坂には延宝年間（1670年代）には80、天保年間（1840年代）には125の藩の蔵屋敷があったとされ」)",
 "fn-137": "no publicly readable source (searched 2026-09-14: a universal negative no page states: \"China Japan no aqueduct arches arcaded aqueduct absent East Asia water supply canals contrast Rome\" and \"aqueduct arches East Asia China did not build arcades water supply history scholarly comparison Roman engineering absent\", with the Wikipedia articles on aqueducts and on the Tamagawa aqueduct read; every readable page describes the Roman arcade on one side and open earth channels, buried wooden and stone conduits and canals on the other, and none states the absence as such)",
 "fn-138": "<a href=\"https://www.mizu.gr.jp/news/221226_report.html\"><code>mizu-no-bunka-mokuhi</code></a> - 「The Kanda Josui had 1.56 km of open channel and a combined 67 km of stone and wooden conduits」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「神田上水が水路部分1.56km・石樋と木樋の総延長67km」); <a href=\"https://www.mizu.gr.jp/news/221226_report.html\"><code>mizu-no-bunka-mokuhi</code></a> - 「The Tamagawa Josui had 43 km of open channel and a combined 84.7 km of stone and wooden conduits」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「玉川上水が水路部分43km・石樋と木樋の総延長84.7km」); <a href=\"https://ktjplus.jp/org/masukosuidou01/masukosuidou07/\"><code>ktjplus-josui-ido</code></a> - 「the number of josui wells across Edo-Tokyo was as many as 8,000」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「上水井戸の数は江戸東京中に8000か所もありました」) (the seminar report attributes its lengths to the Meiji record 東京通志)",
 "fn-139": "<a href=\"https://www.nippon.com/en/guide-to-japan/gu004006/\"><code>nippon-com-suidobashi</code></a> - 「The name Suidōbashi is said to date back to the early Edo period when the bridge straddled a wooden pipe funneling water from the Kōrakuen side to Kanda.」; <a href=\"https://www.nippon.com/en/guide-to-japan/gu004006/\"><code>nippon-com-suidobashi</code></a> - 「The waterway visible in Hiroshige's print is the Kanda Jōsui.」 (the gloss aqueduct bridge is this page's translation of the name)",
 "fn-140": "<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tamagawa_Aqueduct\"><code>tamagawa-aqueduct-enwiki</code></a> - 「The upstream portion from Hamura is largely an open-cut earth channel, dug through the Musashino Plateau.」; <a href=\"https://www.edo-tokyo-museum.or.jp/en/s-exhibition/tamagawajosui/\"><code>edo-tokyo-museum-tamagawa</code></a> - 「The Tamagawa Aqueduct branched off from the middle reach of the Tama River at Hamura to stretch out a length of 43 kilometers.」",
 "fn-141": "<a href=\"https://worksinprogress.co/issue/samurai-city/\"><code>worksinprogress-samurai-city</code></a> - 「Each estate was surrounded by a perimeter wall broken by just one or two richly ornamented gates.」 (the essay describes the daimyo estates of Edo's High City; that no palisade ringed a whole warrior district is this page's reading of the castle-town zoning it cites beside this)",
 "fn-142": "<a href=\"https://zh.wikisource.org/zh-hans/%E5%A4%A7%E6%B8%85%E5%BE%8B%E4%BE%8B/%E5%85%B5%E5%BE%8B\"><code>daqing-luli-yejin</code></a> - 「In all cases of the night curfew in the capital, whoever offends after the bell has fallen silent at the third mark of the first watch and before the bell has stirred at the third mark of the fifth watch shall receive thirty blows of the light bamboo; whoever offends in the second, third or fourth watch shall receive fifty. In the cities and towns of the outer commanderies each penalty is reduced by one degree. In the capital and the outer commanderies alike, those on urgent public business, and households of soldiers or civilians with illness, childbirth or a death, are not within the prohibition」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「凡京城夜禁一更三点钟声已静之后五更三点钟声未动之前犯者笞三十二更三更四更犯者笞五十外郡城镇各减一等其京城外郡因公务急速军民之家有疾病生产死丧不在禁限」); <a href=\"https://www.visitbeijing.com.cn/article/47QkXx9caZ2\"><code>visitbeijing-zhalan</code></a> - 「Each paling was manned by three foot soldiers.」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「栅栏每座设步军三人。」) (the clock hours are this page's conversion of the watches; the code names a bell at both ends of the night, not a drum and a bell)",
 "fn-143": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E8%B7%AF%E5%9C%B0\"><code>roji-jawiki</code></a> - 「It was opened at ake-mutsu and closed at kure-mutsu (about 6 in the evening)」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「明六つ（あけむつ）に開けられ暮六つ（くれむつ）（午後6時頃）に閉められた」); <a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E8%B7%AF%E5%9C%B0\"><code>roji-jawiki</code></a> - 「Its key was held either by the nagaya's landlord or by whoever was on duty for that month, and that person opened and closed the roji-kido」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「その鍵は長屋の大家が持つか、あるいは当番となった者が月番で持ち、その者が路地木戸の開け閉めをし」) (the article calls the gate at an alley mouth a roji-kido and contrasts it with the machi-kido, kept by a paid keeper and shut at about 10 at night)",
 "fn-144": "<a href=\"https://kotobank.jp/word/%E6%9C%A8%E6%88%B8-51154\"><code>kotobank-kido</code></a> - 「It served to prevent criminals from fleeing, and in disturbances such as riots the closing of the kido at these key points sought to stop the trouble spreading to other districts, so its role in maintaining urban public order was large」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「犯罪人の逃亡防止に役だち、打ちこわしなど不穏な事態の際も、この要所の木戸を閉じることにより、他地域への波及を食い止めようとするなど、都市の治安維持の役割が大きかった」); <a href=\"https://kotobank.jp/word/%E6%9C%A8%E6%88%B8-51154\"><code>kotobank-kido</code></a> - 「It was maintained out of the machi's own levy」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「町入用によって維持された」); <a href=\"https://worksinprogress.co/issue/samurai-city/\"><code>worksinprogress-samurai-city</code></a> - 「The Low City was thus divided up by many thousands of internal checkpoints, all of which closed at night.」 (the block paid for its gate out of its own levy and the encyclopedia credits the gates with a large part in keeping order; that the gates closed at night is the English essay's; the word self-governing is this page's summary of the levy)",
 "fn-145": "<a href=\"https://www.mnwhstq.com/whdt_347/202604/t20260417_220969.htm\"><code>mnwhstq-quanzhou-walls</code></a> - 「Within the zicheng a yacheng was further built, forming the three-tier pattern of \"yacheng, zicheng, luocheng\"」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「又在子城内增筑衙城，形成\"衙城、子城、罗城\"三重格局」) (the nesting is recorded for one prefectural seat, Quanzhou, whose gazetteers also name a fourth ring, the yicheng; Tang Yangzhou's two-ring form is the other case read, and the pattern as a rule for prefectural seats rests on those two)",
 "fn-146": "<a href=\"https://zh.wikipedia.org/zh-hans/%E6%98%8E%E6%9C%9D%E5%86%85%E9%98%81\"><code>ming-neige-zhwiki</code></a> - 「The Ming Wenyuan Ge was one of the Grand Secretariat's principal offices; but the Wenyuan Ge of that time did not stand behind today's Wenhua Hall - it stood east of the Meridian Gate, near the Qing-dynasty Grand Secretariat archive.」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「明朝文渊阁为内阁主要办公场所之一，然当时文渊阁的位置并不在今天的文华殿后方，而是在午门之东，清代内阁大库附近。」) (the Six Ministries on the approach are the Corridor of a Thousand Steps note above; the article also says the Secretariat was never a first-rank organ of state, which is why it is called a secretariat here and not a council)",
 "fn-147": "<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Keep\"><code>keep-enwiki</code></a> - 「The corners of square keeps were theoretically vulnerable to siege engines and galleried mining, but before the introduction of the trebuchet at the end of the 12th century, early artillery stood little practical chance of damaging the keeps, and galleried mining was rarely practised.」; <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Keep\"><code>keep-enwiki</code></a> - 「Similarly, the corners of a square keep created dead space that defenders could not fire at, but missile fire in castle sieges was less important until the introduction of the crossbow in the middle of the 12th century.」",
 "fn-148": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E5%B0%8F%E7%94%B0%E5%8E%9F%E5%9F%8E\"><code>odawara-castle-jawiki</code></a> - 「It enclosed the whole town of Odawara, from Mount Hachiman down to the seaward side, with an earthen rampart and dry moat nine kilometers in total length」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「八幡山から海側に至るまで小田原の町全体を総延長9キロメートルの土塁と空堀で取り囲んだものであり」) (the horseshoe is this page's reading of the shape; the article gives the extent and the length)",
 "fn-149": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E6%9B%B2%E8%BC%AA\"><code>kuruwa-jawiki</code></a> - 「in almost every case the form arises from dividing an elongated height - a ridge, a cape, a tongue-shaped terrace - with transverse ditches」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「尾根や岬、舌状台地などの細長い高所を横堀で区切ることでこの形式になる場合がほとんどで」); <a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E6%9B%B2%E8%BC%AA\"><code>kuruwa-jawiki</code></a> - 「a nawabari in which the honmaru is placed with its back to a \"natural defensive work\" such as a lake, a mountain, a river or a cliff, and is enclosed by other baileys on two or three sides」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「本丸を湖沼や山河、絶壁などの『天然の防御設備』を背にして配置し、本丸の周囲の2方向、あるいは3方向を他の曲輪で囲む縄張である。」); <a href=\"https://shirobito.jp/article/615\"><code>shirobito-615-ore</code></a> - 「By setting angles into the line of the rampart it becomes possible to shoot arrows and fire guns from the flank, the enemy's weak side, so that even a few missile weapons are used effectively.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「塁線に角度を設けることによって、敵の弱点である横合いから弓を射かけたり鉄砲を撃ったりすることが可能になり、少ない飛び道具も有効利用できるのです。」) (the same article lists a circular arrangement, the enkaku form, so the classifications are not all angular; the rectangular base of a keep is on no page read and is noted beside this)",
 "fn-150": "no publicly readable source (searched 2026-09-14: the plan shape of a keep's base is on no page read - \"天守 平面 長方形 石垣 折れ 横矢掛かり 隅櫓 直線 城郭\" and \"日本の城 縄張 輪郭式 梯郭式 連郭式 分類 wikipedia\", with the Japanese Wikipedia articles on the kuruwa and on the tenshu read; the latter defines the tenshudai as the raised platform of earth or stone the keep is raised on and says nothing of its shape)",
 "fn-151": "<a href=\"https://xbgjxt.swu.edu.cn/data/article/preview-pdf?doi=10.13718/j.cnki.xdsk.2016.05.023\"><code>chen-2016-song-yamen</code></a> - 「In a Song local yamen the central axis is the backbone of the building complex; the centrality, constraint, cohesion and order expressed by the axial arrangement match exactly the image of the yamen as the local center of administration.」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「宋代地方衙署中的中轴线是衙署建筑群的脊梁骨，中轴线排列所表现出的中心性、约束性、凝聚性和秩序性正合于衙署的地方行政中心的形象。」); <a href=\"https://xinwen.bjd.com.cn/content/s68f22db6d5dee3918d4d209e.html\"><code>bjd-qing-yamen</code></a> - 「The layout is symmetrical, rational and compact, with principal and secondary clearly distinguished」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「布局对称、合理、紧凑，主次分明」); <a href=\"https://xinwen.bjd.com.cn/content/s68f22db6d5dee3918d4d209e.html\"><code>bjd-qing-yamen</code></a> - 「Six courtyard groups in all, 85 rooms」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「共6组四合院，85间房屋」)",
 "fn-152": "<a href=\"https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E5%91%A8%E7%A6%AE/%E5%86%AC%E5%AE%98%E8%80%83%E5%B7%A5%E8%A8%98\"><code>kaogongji-wikisource</code></a> - 「The builder lays out the capital: nine li square, with three gates on each side. Within the city, nine north-south and nine east-west thoroughfares, each thoroughfare nine chariot-tracks wide. Ancestral temple on the left, altar of soil on the right; the court in front, the market behind; market and court each one fu square.」 (translated from the Classical Chinese by this project; original: 「匠人營國。方九里，旁三門。國中九經九緯，經塗九軌。左祖右社，面朝後市，市朝一夫。」); <a href=\"https://m.thepaper.cn/newsDetail_forward_1270378\"><code>thepaper-city-walls</code></a> - 「Many people put this phenomenon down mainly to the influence of the \"round heaven, square earth\" view, but that view has no direct evidence at all」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「对于这一现象很多人将其归结于主要受到\"天圆地方\"观点的影响，不过这一观点并没有什么直接的证据」); <a href=\"https://m.thepaper.cn/newsDetail_forward_1270378\"><code>thepaper-city-walls</code></a> - 「It is better to acknowledge that in city planning the square was simply the easiest form to build」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「不如承认城市规划中方形最便于建设」)",
 "fn-153": "<a href=\"https://www.jiningmuseum.com/list/article_details.do?articleId=1932\"><code>jining-museum-qiandao</code></a> - 「Meng Jian, Surveillance Commissioner of Eastern Zhejiang, raised an earth-embankment towpath there in the tenth year of Yuanhe (815)」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「浙东观察使孟简于元和十年（815年）在当地筑起了土堤型纤道」); <a href=\"https://zh.wikipedia.org/zh-hans/%E6%B5%99%E4%B8%9C%E8%BF%90%E6%B2%B3%E7%BA%A4%E9%81%93\"><code>zhedong-qiandao-zhwiki</code></a> - 「close to a hundred li in total length」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「全长近百里」); <a href=\"https://zh.wikipedia.org/zh-hans/%E6%B5%99%E4%B8%9C%E8%BF%90%E6%B2%B3%E7%BA%A4%E9%81%93\"><code>zhedong-qiandao-zhwiki</code></a> - 「The Eastern Zhejiang Canal towpath has two forms, one side resting on the bank and both sides standing in water; the latter divides again into solid towpath and stone-pier towpath.」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「浙东运河纤道具有单面靠岸和双面临水两种形式，后者又可分为实体纤道和石墩纤道。」) (the encyclopedia says Meng Jian carried out a large reconstruction in 815 where the museum says he built it; the protected stretch is 7.5 km)",
 "fn-154": "no publicly readable source (searched 2026-09-14: the height of the deck above the water is on no page read - \"紹興 纤道 石板 桥墩 高出水面 古纤道\" and \"古纤道 桥 支流 河口 跨越 涵洞 纤道 拱桥 便于船只通行\", with the Chinese Wikipedia article on the towpath read (its two forms, no dimensions) and the Keqiao district and Zhejiang water-resources pages timing out; the search summaries of those two pages give three stone beams 0.49 to 0.52 m WIDE with the deck about a meter above the water, so the half-meter here may be the slab's width read as a height, and the figure stands unverified until the pages can be read)",
 "fn-155": "<a href=\"https://spot.colorado.edu/~shiue/p1406_s.pdf\"><code>shiue-2002</code></a> - 「Over long distances, junks may have had a 9–10-fold cost advantage over the two primary forms of land transportation, human and animal carriage (Andrew Watson, 1972).」; <a href=\"https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/logisticssociety1995/2003/11/2003_11_121/_pdf/-char/ja\"><code>kobayashi-2003-kasen-shuun</code></a> - 「For this reason, in order to transport annual tribute rice and the like in bulk from every region to Edo, river boat transport - which carried greater volume than land transport - was much used.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「このため各地から江戸へ年貢米などを大量に輸送するため、陸上輸送よりも輸送量の多い河川舟運が多く利用された。」) (the cost gap is the sources'; that a road beside a navigable river is therefore redundant is this page's reasoning from it)",
 "fn-156": "<a href=\"https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/journalhs1990/14/0/14_0_125/_pdf/-char/ja\"><code>matsumura-1994-oigawa</code></a> - 「In the Edo period not only was no bridge built over the Oi River, the establishment of a ferry was not permitted either. The traveler therefore had no option but to be carried across by human power.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「江戸時代の大井川には橋が架けられなかったばかりでなく、渡船の設置も認められなかった。したがって、旅行者は人力に頼って渡してもらうより他はなかった。」); <a href=\"https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/journalhs1990/14/0/14_0_125/_pdf/-char/ja\"><code>matsumura-1994-oigawa</code></a> - 「It is generally accepted that the Oigawa wading system has been developed under the military purpose of defending an old capital Edo, or under the political purpose of supporting relay stations. However, it can be pointed out that both technical and socio-economic factors affected even more significantly in the process of each river-crossing style formation.」; <a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E5%A4%A7%E4%BA%95%E5%B7%9D\"><code>oigawa-jawiki</code></a> - 「Recent research also holds that the old Oi River, with its great volume and swift current, was unsuited to bridging, and that the system existed to protect the enormous profits of the river offices and post towns that the crossing supported」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「近年の研究では、昔の大井川は水量が多く流れも急だったため、架橋には向かなかった、川越による川会所や宿場町の莫大な利益を守るためであった、とも言われている」) (that Japanese roads did not follow rivers as a rule is this page's own generalization; the Oi-kawa is the case read)",
 "fn-157": "<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_city_wall\"><code>chinese-city-wall-enwiki</code></a> - 「Large and elaborate towers, called corner towers ... were placed where two walls joined (i.e. at corners). These were significantly higher than the wall itself, and gave defenders a bird's eye view over both the city and its surroundings.」; <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_city_wall\"><code>chinese-city-wall-enwiki</code></a> - 「Most imperial capitals and many important cities in the north had the walls of rectangular shape. In areas of rugged relief, however, a square form was usually replaced by one of irregular shape.」 (the corner towers and the rectangular plans are the article's; the horse-face bastions and the formwork argument are noted beside this as unread)",
 "fn-158": "no publicly readable source (searched 2026-09-14: the horse-face bastion (馬面) and the claim that rammed-earth formwork and stone revetment want straight runs are on no page read - \"Chinese city wall mamian horse-face bastion 马面 城墙 角楼 直线 夯土 防御 死角\" and \"城墙 马面 间距 六十步 弩射 两侧 夹击 墨子 备城门 研究 论文\"; the Architectura Sinica glossary refused the fetch and the Chinese Wikipedia article on the bastion is gone, so the definition came only from search summaries; Chen Gui's Song manual 守城錄, which sets a bastion every sixty paces, is the primary text to read)",
 "fn-159": "<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fujian_tulou\"><code>fujian-tulou-enwiki</code></a> - 「All rooms were built the same size with the same grade of material, same exterior decoration, same style of windows and doors, and there was no 'penthouse' for 'higher echelons.'」; <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fujian_tulou\"><code>fujian-tulou-enwiki</code></a> - 「Tulous were usually occupied by one large family clan of several generations; some larger tulou had more than one family clan.」; <a href=\"https://zh.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E7%A6%8F%E5%BB%BA%E5%9C%9F%E6%A5%BC\"><code>fujian-tulou-zhwiki</code></a> - 「The square tulou is the most widespread of the enclosed buildings.」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「方樓是圍樓中最為普及。」) (the word wedge is this page's; the pages speak of rooms of equal size)",
 "fn-160": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E8%94%B5%E5%89%8D\"><code>kuramae-jawiki</code></a> - 「The Asakusa Okura made eight channels on the right bank of the Sumida, counted first, second and so on from upstream, and many rice storehouses stood in rows facing them.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「浅草御蔵は、隅田川の右岸に上流から一番、二番と数える8本の堀を作り、それに面した多くの米蔵が連なった。」); <a href=\"https://wheatbaku.exblog.jp/30482848/\"><code>wheatbaku-asakusa-okura</code></a> - 「Inside the storehouse ground there were eight boat-entry channels, so that rice sent by boat from the shogunal domains could be unloaded.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「御蔵内には、天領から船で送られた米を荷揚げするため、８本の舟入堀がありました。」) (the channels and the rows facing them are the pages'; the raised floor, the stone revetment and the flood reasoning are noted beside this as unread)",
 "fn-161": "no publicly readable source (searched 2026-09-14: the raised floor and stone revetment of the Kuramae storehouses, and flood as the reason for them, are on no page read - \"浅草御蔵 蔵前 隅田川 河岸 石垣 荷揚げ 堀 舟 米蔵 高床\" and \"江戸 米蔵 板倉 高床 校倉 湿気 床を高く 御蔵 構造\"; the Japanese Wikipedia article on Kuramae and an Edo-topography blog describe the channels and the storehouses and say nothing of flood; a raised floor is attested for the ancient storehouse type, a different building from an Edo rice kura; the shogunal topography 御府内備考 and the Taito ward history are the works to read)",
 "fn-162": "<a href=\"https://kotobank.jp/word/%E5%AF%BA%E7%94%BA-101789\"><code>kotobank-teramachi</code></a> - 「Temples were placed collectively and continuously at the key points of the outer perimeter.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「外郭の要所に寺院が集団的・連続的に配置された。」); <a href=\"https://kotobank.jp/word/%E5%AF%BA%E7%94%BA-101789\"><code>kotobank-teramachi</code></a> - 「Given the function of their large building groups and wide precincts, they were placed mainly with their military significance in an emergency in mind」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「大規模な建築群と広い境内の機能から、主として非常時における軍事的意義を考慮して配置されていた」); <a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E5%AF%BA%E7%94%BA\"><code>teramachi-jawiki</code></a> - 「Before the Edo period, with the intent of forming a defensive line in the event of an enemy attack, temples and graveyards were gathered at the outer edge of the town in many castle towns.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「江戸時代以前、敵襲に際して防衛線とする意図で、多くの城下町において寺院や墓地が市街の外縁にまとめられた。」)",
 "fn-163": "no publicly readable source (searched 2026-09-14: that every temple compound in a teramachi was its own walled enclosure, wall against wall, is on no page read - \"寺町 寺院 塀 土塀 防衛 城下町 配置 一列 惣構\"; the kotobank and Wikipedia entries on the teramachi speak of wide precincts placed collectively and continuously, never of each precinct's wall or of walls touching)",
 "fn-164": "<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pingyao\"><code>pingyao-enwiki</code></a> - 「Pingyao is located on the east bank of the Fen River near the southwestern edge of the Taiyuan Basin」; <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pingyao\"><code>pingyao-enwiki</code></a> - 「A 4 m wide and 4 m (13 ft) deep moat can be found just outside the walls.」; <a href=\"https://zh.wikipedia.org/zh-hans/%E5%B9%B3%E9%81%A5%E5%9F%8E%E5%A2%99\"><code>pingyao-chengqiang-zhwiki</code></a> - 「The southern city wall was built along the Zhongdu River, which is why it appears somewhat winding.」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「南侧城墙沿中都河而建，故略显蜿蜒。」) (neither article calls the moat a complete circuit; both say it lies outside the walls)",
 "fn-165": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E5%A0%80\"><code>hori-jawiki</code></a> - 「A wet moat was sometimes not one continuous water surface but divided by earthen banks (weirs), giving a difference in water level between the parts. This is called mitochigai.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「水堀は、水面をひと続きとせず土居（堰）で区切り、水位に高低差を付けることもあった。これを水戸違い（みとちがい）と呼ぶ。」); <a href=\"https://blog.kojodan.jp/entry/2020/09/25/180000\"><code>kojodan-mizubori</code></a> - 「Also, when a moat was dug, water sometimes welled up in it naturally.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「また、堀を掘れば自然に水が湧いてくることもありました。」)",
 "fn-166": "no publicly readable source (searched 2026-09-14: the siege reasoning is on no page read - \"城 堀 水源 湧水 地下水 水堀 城郭\", with the Shirobito column on wet moats and the Japanese Wikipedia article on the moat read; both list groundwater, rain and water drawn from a river, lake or sea as sources, and neither says a castle preferred groundwater because a supply can be cut)",
 "fn-167": "<a href=\"https://shirobito.jp/article/906\"><code>shirobito-906-mizubori</code></a> - 「There were cases in which a wet moat came about from water welling up naturally from underground, from water no longer needed inside the castle and let out, or from natural rainwater collecting - and cases in which, to strengthen the castle's defenses, water was deliberately drawn in from a nearby river, lake or the sea.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「自然に地下から湧き出る水や、城内で使いいらなくなって流した水や、自然の雨水が溜まって水堀になった場合と、城の守りを固めるために、近くにある河川（かせん）・湖（みずうみ）・海からわざわざ引いてくるケースがありました。」); <a href=\"https://www.my-kagawa.jp/point/2840/\"><code>takamatsu-my-kagawa</code></a> - 「Into that moat seawater from the Seto Inland Sea is drawn, and fish such as sea bream swim in it.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「そのお堀には、瀬戸内海の海水が引かれ、鯛などの魚が泳いでいます。」); <a href=\"http://imabarijo.blogspot.com/2014/08/blog-post_30.html\"><code>imabarijo-blog</code></a> - 「One of Imabari Castle's distinguishing features is that seawater flows into and out of the moat.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「今治城の特徴の一つに、堀に海水が出入りしていることがあります。」)",
 "fn-168": "no publicly readable source (searched 2026-09-14: an exhaustive inventory of which enclosures a period city did and did not wall is on no page read - \"城下町 町人地 塀がない 武家屋敷 土塀 長屋門 区画 囲い 江戸 町割り 防御\" and \"寺町 寺院 塀 土塀 防衛 城下町 配置 一列 惣構\", with the Japanese Wikipedia articles on the castle town, the samurai residence and the temple town, the kotobank teramachi entry and two castle sites read; each describes the districts and particular enclosures, none states the only ones; the positive half - the castle, the walled yashiki and the sogamae - is cited elsewhere on this page, and the word only is this page's inference)",
 "fn-169": "<a href=\"https://kotobank.jp/word/%E9%96%80%E5%89%8D%E7%94%BA-143199\"><code>kotobank-monzenmachi-143199</code></a> - 「a settlement formed in street-village fashion chiefly along both sides of the pilgrimage road」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「参詣道路の両側を中心に街村状に形成された集落」); <a href=\"https://kotobank.jp/word/%E9%96%80%E5%89%8D%E7%94%BA-143199\"><code>kotobank-monzenmachi-143199</code></a> - 「a town in which inns and commerce serving pilgrims and sightseers developed and became the principal livelihood」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「参拝人・遊覧客を対象とする宿屋や商業が発達して、それらを主たる生業とする町」); <a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E6%9C%A8%E6%88%B8%E7%95%AA\"><code>kidoban-jawiki</code></a> - 「In Edo and in many castle towns, kido were installed, and they were to be closed at night」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「江戸をはじめ、多くの城下には木戸が設けられており、夜は閉じられることになっていた」) (the same reference contrasts the monzen with the jinaicho, the temple town that was ringed by a moat and rampart, so the enclosed form is a different type)",
 "fn-170": "no publicly readable source (searched 2026-09-14: \"玉川上水 開渠 幅 間 水路 幅員 尺\", \"玉川上水 開渠 川幅 二間 三間 掘割 断面 寸法\" and \"江戸 上水 開渠 幅 一間 二間 三間 神田上水 千川上水 水路幅\", with the Japanese Wikipedia article on the Tamagawa aqueduct read (its only ken figure is a 1739 felling ban three ken wide on each bank, a margin and not the cut), Kodaira city's pages, and two JStage and adeac PDFs that fetched as images; the search summaries alone give the original cut as 4 to 6 ken on the upper reach and 2 to 4 on the lower, widened by 3 ken in 1670, which would put the figure here too low, so it stands unverified until the Tokyo Waterworks History Museum's book or the JSIDRE article on the aqueduct can be read)",
 "fn-171": "<a href=\"https://www.cchfound.com/product/2934.html\"><code>cchfound-guqiandao</code></a> - 「The path is 1.5 m wide」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「道宽1.5米」); <a href=\"https://www.cchfound.com/product/2934.html\"><code>cchfound-guqiandao</code></a> - 「the deck is laid flat from stone slabs about 3 m long and about 0.5 m wide」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「长约3米、宽约0.5米的石板平铺而成桥面」) (the same page gives the piers' span as 2 m, which is not the deck's width)",
 "fn-172": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E6%9D%B1%E5%A4%A7%E5%AF%BA%E5%A4%A7%E4%BB%8F%E6%AE%BF\"><code>todaiji-daibutsuden-jawiki</code></a> - 「57.5 m wide at the front, 50.5 m deep, 49.1 m high to the ridge」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「正面の幅57.5m、奥行き50.5m、棟までの高さ49.1m」); <a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E6%9D%B1%E5%A4%A7%E5%AF%BA%E5%A4%A7%E4%BB%8F%E6%AE%BF\"><code>todaiji-daibutsuden-jawiki</code></a> - 「29 jo wide (about 85.8 m), 17 jo deep (about 50.3 m), 12 jo 6 shaku high (about 37 m)」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「幅29丈（約85.8m）、奥行き17丈（約50.3m）、高さ12丈6尺（約37m）」); <a href=\"https://www.chion-in.or.jp/mieido/story/story_06.php\"><code>chion-in-mieido</code></a> - 「45 meters across the front and 35 meters deep, it boasts a size among the very largest in Japan.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「間口45メートル、奥行き35メートル、日本でも指折りの大きさを誇ります。」) (the standing Todaiji hall, 57.5 m, is the ~190 ft end and Chion-in's 45 m the ~150 ft end; the founding hall's 85.8 m is why the ceiling is stated for standing halls only)",
 "fn-173": "<a href=\"https://wako226.exblog.jp/239926652/\"><code>wako226-hairyo-yashiki</code></a> - 「The size of a granted residence followed, by the regulation keyed to stipend, 2,500 tsubo for a daimyo of 10,000-20,000 koku, 3,500 tsubo at 30,000-40,000, 4,500 tsubo at 40,000-50,000, and 5,100 tsubo at 50,000-60,000. Further it was set at 5,600 tsubo for 60,000-70,000 koku, 6,600 tsubo for 80,000-90,000, and 7,200 tsubo for 100,000-150,000.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「拝領屋敷の広さは、知行高による規定から、1～2万石の大名は2500坪、3～4万石で3500坪、４～5万石で4500坪、5～6万石で5100坪である。さらに6～7万石で5600坪、8～9万石で6600坪、10～15万石で7200坪とされていた。」); <a href=\"https://wako226.exblog.jp/239926652/\"><code>wako226-hairyo-yashiki</code></a> - 「In practice they formally borrowed from the shogunate residence plots larger than the standard regulation」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「実際は標準規定よりも広い屋敷用地を形式上幕府から借り受けていた」) (a walking group's summary of the bakufu's table, and it notes that real plots ran larger than the standard)",
 "fn-174": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E7%91%9E%E9%B3%B3%E6%AE%BF\"><code>zuihoden-jawiki</code></a> - 「It stands on Kyogamine, which faces the site of the honmaru of Sendai castle across a meander of the Hirose river.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「広瀬川の蛇行部を挟んで、仙台城の本丸跡と向かい合う経ヶ峯に位置する。」); <a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E7%91%9E%E9%B3%B3%E6%AE%BF\"><code>zuihoden-jawiki</code></a> - 「Around the Zuihoden are mausolea and attached buildings connected with the Date - the Kansenden, mausoleum of Date Tadamune; the Zennoden, mausoleum of Date Tsunamune; the Myounkai-byo; and the Okosama-gobyo」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「瑞鳳殿の周辺には伊達忠宗の霊廟である感仙殿（かんせんでん）、伊達綱宗の霊廟である善応殿（ぜんのうでん）、妙雲界廟（みょううんかいびょう）、御子様御廟（おこさまごびょう）といった伊達氏に関連する霊廟や付属施設がある」); <a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E7%91%9E%E9%B3%B3%E6%AE%BF\"><code>zuihoden-jawiki</code></a> - 「intending the preservation of Kyogamine, which, though its historic buildings burned, escaped the felling of its trees」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「歴史的建造物が焼失したものの樹木の伐採は逃れた経ヶ峯の保存を期し」)",
 "fn-175": "<a href=\"https://zh.wikipedia.org/zh-hans/%E6%B1%9F%E5%AE%81%E7%BB%87%E9%80%A0%E8%A1%99%E9%97%A8\"><code>jiangning-zhizao-zhwiki</code></a> - 「Together with the Suzhou weaving office and the Hangzhou weaving office, they are collectively called the Three Jiangnan Weaving Offices」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「与苏州织造、杭州织造，统称为江南三织造」); <a href=\"https://zh.wikipedia.org/zh-hans/%E6%B1%9F%E5%AE%81%E7%BB%87%E9%80%A0%E8%A1%99%E9%97%A8\"><code>jiangning-zhizao-zhwiki</code></a> - 「All great-red python satin, great-red satin, gold-leaf brocade, fringe and the like were assigned to the Jiangning weaving office to produce. Imitation silk gauze, Hangzhou silk and the like, the Hangzhou weaving office produced. Blue cotton cloth and the like, of which thirty thousand bolts were needed each year, the Suzhou weaving office produced」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「凡大红蟒缎、大红缎、片金、折缨等项，派江宁织造承办。仿丝绫、杭绸等项，杭州织造承办。毛青布等项，每年需用三万疋内，苏州织造承办」)",
 "fn-176": "<a href=\"https://www.nippon.com/ja/guide-to-japan/gu004020/\"><code>nippon-com-kanda-konyacho</code></a> - 「The town's name comes from the dyers who, from the early Edo period, stood eave to eave dyeing cotton with indigo.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「江戸時代初期より藍で木綿を染める紺屋が軒を連ねていたのが町名の由来となった。」); <a href=\"https://www.nippon.com/ja/guide-to-japan/gu004020/\"><code>nippon-com-kanda-konyacho</code></a> - 「The bolts of cloth bleached out on fine days were as beautiful as banners, and the sight of them ranged in rows was famous in Hiroshige's day.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「晴天の日に晒（さら）されていた反物が幟（のぼり）のように美しく、それが連なる光景は広重の時代には有名だったらしい。」); <a href=\"https://www.yk-kankou.jp/spotDetail51.html\"><code>yk-kankou-konya</code></a> - 「Konya-machi, where the dyers gathered. A water channel runs down the middle of the street and gives it its particular character. This water flowed out of the castle's moat, and dyed cloth and thread were rinsed in it.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「染物屋さんが集まっていた紺屋町。道筋の中央には水路が通り独特の雰囲気をかもしだしている。この水はお城の堀から流れ出ており、染めた布や糸を晒していた。」)",
 "fn-177": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/小塚原刑場\"><code>kozukappara-jawiki</code></a> - 「In total more than 200,000 convicts were put to death between its founding and its abolition」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「創設から廃止までの間に合計で20万人以上の罪人が死刑された」); <a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/小塚原刑場\"><code>kozukappara-jawiki</code></a> - 「The bodies were left in the open, not buried, merely covered over with earth」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「遺体は野ざらしのまま埋葬せずに土を被せるだけで」); <a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/小塚原刑場\"><code>kozukappara-jawiki</code></a> - 「In the early nineteenth century it became a great cremation and burial ground for the north of Edo」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「19世紀初頭に江戸の北の一大火葬埋葬場となった」)",
 "fn-178": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E4%BB%8A%E6%88%B8%E7%84%BC\"><code>imado-yaki-jawiki</code></a> - 「According to Kanamori Tokusui's Honcho toki kosho, in the closing years of the shogunate there were some fifty households producing Imado ware」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「金森得水『本朝陶器攷證』によれば、幕末期には今戸焼を生産する家が約50軒ほどあったという」); <a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E4%BA%AC%E7%84%BC\"><code>kyoyaki-jawiki</code></a> - 「Ceramic kiln houses were concentrated in the neighborhood of Sanjo Awataguchi, where Awata ware was produced.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「三条粟田口界隈に陶磁器の窯元が集中し、粟田焼が生産されていた。」); <a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E4%BA%AC%E7%84%BC\"><code>kyoyaki-jawiki</code></a> - 「The kilns stood chiefly in Higashiyama, with others scattered in Rakuto and Rakuhoku.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「窯の所在地は東山が中心で、このほかに洛東や洛北に点在した。」) (where the clay and the fuel came in is this page's reading of the siting; the pages give the concentration and the counts)",
 "fn-179": "<a href=\"https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/jjsidre/77/7/77_559/_article/-char/ja\"><code>jsidre-bansui-2009</code></a> - 「\"bansui\", by which simultaneous continuous distribution in normal times is sometimes switched over at times of abnormal drought」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「平常時の同時連続配水を異常渇水時に切り替えることがある「番水」」); <a href=\"https://www.city.kiyosu.aichi.jp/kurashi_joho/seikatsu_kankyo/nogyo/tsusui_R7.html\"><code>kiyosu-haisui-schedule</code></a> - 「water supply begins from Tuesday 21 April」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「4月21日（火曜日）から　通水開始」); <a href=\"https://www.city.kiyosu.aichi.jp/kurashi_joho/seikatsu_kankyo/nogyo/tsusui_R7.html\"><code>kiyosu-haisui-schedule</code></a> - 「water off, 07:00 Monday 28 September」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「断水 9月28日（月曜日）7時」) (both are present-day irrigation practice; the paper says rotation is the drought regime and continuous supply the normal one, which is why the sentence no longer calls rotation the rule)",
 "fn-180": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E5%B7%9D%E8%B6%8A%E4%BA%94%E6%B2%B3%E5%B2%B8\"><code>kawagoe-gokashi-jawiki</code></a> - 「The Kawagoe Five Kashi were the five landings - Ogi, Kami-shin, Ushiko, Shimo-shin and Terao - set up on the upper Shingashi river, and functioned as the outer port of the castle town of Kawagoe.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「川越五河岸は、新河岸川の上流に設置された扇・上新・牛子・下新・寺尾の5つの河岸場で、城下町・川越の外港として機能した。」); <a href=\"https://smtrc.jp/town-archives/city/kawagoe/p03.html\"><code>smtrc-kawagoe-shuun</code></a> - 「Down to the early Edo period Kawagoe's outer port was the Oibukuro kashi and others on the Irima river east of the plateau, but a belt of low wet ground lay between them and the castle town, which made carrying heavy goods inconvenient.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「江戸初期までの川越の外港は、台地の東側にある「入間川」の「老袋河岸」などが使われていたが、城下町との間には低湿地帯が拡がっており重量物の運搬などには不便であった。」)",
 "fn-181": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/河岸\"><code>kashi-jawiki</code></a> - 「From the Edo period on, merchants running wholesale houses and their storehouses gathered at a kashi, forming a single commercial settlement.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「江戸時代に入ると河岸には問屋を商う商人やその蔵が集まり、一つの商業集落を形成していた。」); <a href=\"https://kotobank.jp/word/%E6%B2%B3%E5%B2%B8-459508\"><code>kotobank-kashi</code></a> - 「carried out the loading, landing and storage of goods and the issuing of bills of lading, and received commission and yard fees」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「荷物の船積み、船揚げ、保管等や送状の発行を行って口銭・庭銭を受け取った」); <a href=\"https://www.chuoku-machikadotenjikan.jp/feature/special09_nihonbashi05.html\"><code>chuoku-nihonbashi-kashi</code></a> - 「Both banks prospered as the rice kashi, the small-boat kashi, the salt kashi and so on.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「両岸は米河岸、小舟河岸、塩河岸等として繁栄。」) (a timber kashi was on no page read, so the example is the small-boat kashi the ward's page names)",
 "fn-182": "<a href=\"https://www.mizu.gr.jp/kikanshi/no32/07.html\"><code>mizu-no-bunka-32-yanagawa</code></a> - 「Yanagawa is an alluvial plain built from the silt the Chikugo river carried. On the Ariake sea, where the tidal range reaches 6 m, wide tideflats appear at low water.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「柳川は、筑後川が運んだ土砂でつくられた沖積平野。最大6mの干満の差がある有明海では、干潮時には大きな干潟が出現する。」); <a href=\"https://www.mizu.gr.jp/kikanshi/no32/07.html\"><code>mizu-no-bunka-32-yanagawa</code></a> - 「The water of the moat-channels is drawn upstream of a tide barrier called the Isodori weir, so no tide enters them.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「堀割の水は、磯鳥堰（いそどりせき）という防潮堤の上流で取水しているので潮が入っていない。」); <a href=\"https://www.nippon.com/ja/guide-to-japan/gu900102/\"><code>nippon-com-matsue-horikawa</code></a> - 「a \"water city\" through which the waterways called the Matsue Horikawa - spreading out from the moat of Matsue Castle, a national treasure - run in every direction」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「国宝・松江城の堀から広がる水路「松江堀川（通称：堀川）」が縦横に走る「水の都」」)",
 "fn-183": "<a href=\"https://www.qiaokou.gov.cn/qkgk/hghh/201708/t20170828_117732.shtml\"><code>qiaokou-hanzheng-street</code></a> - 「Hankou's earliest street was River Street; then came Main Street, Inner Street and Lane Street」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「汉口最初的街道是河街，然后有了正街、内街、夹街」); <a href=\"https://www.qiaokou.gov.cn/qkgk/hghh/201708/t20170828_117732.shtml\"><code>qiaokou-hanzheng-street</code></a> - 「merchant boats shuttled ceaselessly on the river, and wharves large and small stood in rows along the bank」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「江面上商船穿梭不息，岸边大小码头林立」); <a href=\"https://zh.wikipedia.org/zh-hans/%E6%B1%89%E6%AD%A3%E8%A1%97\"><code>hanzheng-jie-zhwiki</code></a> - 「In the Ming, because the Han river shifted its course, the rising town of Hankou formed along the north bank of the new channel; wharves stood in rows and joined one another, forming a ten-li street running east to west.」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「明代由于汉水改道，沿着汉水新河道的北岸形成新兴的汉口镇，码头林立，并相互连接，形成横贯东西的十里长街。」)",
 "fn-184": "no publicly readable source (searched 2026-09-14: the contrast as a rule - a boat-entry basin belonging to flat delta ground where the canal is the street grid - is on no page read; \"汉口 沿江 码头 河街 商业区 长江 装卸 货物 沿岸 仓库 清代\" found the river-city half, which the next sentence carries, and nothing stating the canal-country half)",
 "fn-185": "no publicly readable source (searched 2026-09-14: a rule of this shape is on no page read - \"graveyard cremation ground distance outside city gate premodern Japan Edo Kyoto burial ground located just outside the wall\" and \"鳥辺野 蓮台野 化野 風葬 火葬 葬送の地 平安京 三大葬地\"; the pages read describe each site singly, and the setback band is this page's own measurement over the cases cited above, one of which - the Rokudō distance - is itself on no page)",
 "fn-186": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E5%85%AD%E9%81%93%E7%8F%8D%E7%9A%87%E5%AF%BA\"><code>rokudo-chinnoji-jawiki</code></a> - 「The vicinity of this temple's site is the entrance to Toribeno, the cremation ground of Heian-kyō, and was held to be the boundary between this world and the other world; it was called the \"Rokudō crossroads\".」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「当寺の所在地付近は、平安京の火葬地であった鳥部野（鳥辺野）の入口にあたり、現世と他界の境にあたると考えられ、「六道の辻」と呼ばれた。」); <a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E9%A2%A8%E8%91%AC\"><code>fuso-jawiki</code></a> - 「From the Heian period onward, in Kyoto, Katabiragatsuji, Adashino, Toribeno and Rendaino were grounds of the ancient exposure burial, grounds of funerary disposal.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「平安時代以降、京都では帷子辻（かたびらがつじ）、化野（あだしの）、鳥辺野（とりべの）、蓮台野（れんだいの）が、古来の風葬の地、葬送の地であった。」); <a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/鳥辺野\"><code>toribeno-jawiki</code></a> - 「was cremated at that same place」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「同地で荼毘に付された」) (exposure, cremation and burial are each attested for Toribeno on the pages read; for Rendaino the pages give exposure and the gate-temple)",
 "fn-187": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E5%BC%95%E6%8E%A5%E5%AF%BA_(%E4%BA%AC%E9%83%BD%E5%B8%82)\"><code>injoji-jawiki</code></a> - 「As its name says, this temple stands at the entrance of Rendaino, one of the three great graveyards of the old Kyoto - Adashino, Toribeno and Rendaino.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「その名の通りこの寺は、かつての京都の3大墓地であった化野、鳥辺野、蓮台野（れんだいの）の一つである蓮台野の入口に立っている。」); <a href=\"https://souda-kyoto.jp/guide/spot/senbonenmado.html\"><code>souda-kyoto-enmado</code></a> - 「Because it stands at the entrance of Rendaino, one of the funerary grounds of Heian-kyō, it has long been known as a place leading through to the other world and as the fundamental hall for welcoming the spirits」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「平安京の葬送地のひとつ蓮台野の入口にあたることから、あの世へ通じる場所として、古くからお精霊迎えの根本道場として知られており」)",
 "fn-188": "<a href=\"https://imari-ookawachiyama.com/history/\"><code>imari-okawachiyama</code></a> - 「In 1675 the Nabeshima domain moved its domain kiln to Okawachiyama and, in order to prevent the outflow of its distinctive techniques and of its craftsmen, set up a barrier and imposed a strict system of control.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「一六七五年、鍋島藩は藩窯を大川内山へ移し、独自の技法や職人の流出を防ぐために関所を設けて厳格な管理体制を敷きました。」)",
 "fn-189": "<a href=\"https://zh.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E7%90%89%E7%92%83%E5%8E%82\"><code>liulichang-zhwiki</code></a> - 「Here was established the Liulichang, one of the five great works belonging to the Ministry of Works (Shenmuchang, Damuchang, Heiyaochang, Liulichang, Taijichang)」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「在此地设立工部所属五大厂（神木厂，大木厂，黑窑厂，琉璃厂，台基厂）之一的琉璃厂」); <a href=\"https://zh.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E7%90%89%E7%92%83%E5%8E%82\"><code>liulichang-zhwiki</code></a> - 「In the Yuan dynasty an official kiln was opened here, firing glazed tile. From the Ming construction of the inner city, because palaces were being built, the scale of the official kiln was enlarged」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「元朝的时候这里开设了官窑，烧制琉璃瓦。自明代建设内城时，因为修建宫殿，就扩大了官窑的规模」); <a href=\"https://zh.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E7%90%89%E7%92%83%E5%8E%82\"><code>liulichang-zhwiki</code></a> - 「The extent of the Liulichang at that time reached north to the west bank of the inner-city moat and south to the site of today's Beijing Normal University.」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「当时琉璃厂的范围北至内城护城河西河沿，南至今北京师范大学位置。」); <a href=\"https://www.visitbeijing.com.cn/article/47yYgUFzCMj\"><code>visitbeijing-wudachang</code></a> - 「The function of the Liulichang was 'to fire bricks and tiles and vessels for the inner court'」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「琉璃厂的职能为'烧造砖瓦及内府器用'」) (that structural ceramic follows the construction site is this page's reading of why the works stood where they did)",
 "fn-190": "<a href=\"https://xinwen.bjd.com.cn/content/s650bf36be4b0d5bd075f4be1.html\"><code>bjd-zhonggulou</code></a> - 「Each night they were closed at the 'setting of the watch' and opened at the 'lightening of the watch'. By the old rule these nine city gates each hung a sounding instrument for marking the hour, and when they heard the bell and drum of the Bell and Drum Towers at the 'setting' and the 'lightening' of the watch, each gate struck its instrument ... and closed or opened the city gate.」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「每晚'定更'时关闭，至'亮更'时开启。按老规矩，这九个城门，都悬挂一个报钟点的响器，当听到钟鼓楼'定更'和'亮更'的钟鼓时，各城门就打点...关闭或开启城门。」) (the city gates; whether the lane palings closed on the same signal is not on the page read)",
 "fn-191": "<a href=\"https://www.visitbeijing.com.cn/article/47QkXx9caZ2\"><code>visitbeijing-zhalan</code></a> - 「In the first year of Hongzhi of the Ming (1488), the Xiaozong emperor ordered palings set up in the great streets and small lanes within the capital and in the suburbs outside the gates, opened early and closed late, to maintain the capital's public order.」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「明弘治元年（1488年），孝宗皇帝下令在京城内及关厢地区的大街小巷设立栅栏，早启晚闭，用于维护京城的治安。」); <a href=\"https://www.visitbeijing.com.cn/article/47QkXx9caZ2\"><code>visitbeijing-zhalan</code></a> - 「At that time the inner city of Beijing alone had more than a thousand palings, not counting the hundred and more within the imperial city.」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「当时仅北京内城就设有栅栏一千多座，这里还不包括皇城在内的一百多处栅栏。」) (the Chinese mesh is the page's, from a Ming edict of 1488; the Edo mesh and the end of the ward walls are cited in the paragraphs below; that no continuous fence stood is this page's inference from both)",
 "fn-192": "<a href=\"https://www.hirosakipark.jp/707.html\"><code>hirosakipark-tenshu</code></a> - 「a first-story plan of 5 ken by 6 ken」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「5間×6間の初層平面」); <a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E5%BC%98%E5%89%8D%E5%9F%8E\"><code>hirosaki-castle-jawiki</code></a> - 「At its original construction its scale reached 612 m east-west by 947 m north-south, a total area of 385,200 square meters.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「創建当初の規模は東西612メートル、南北947メートル、総面積38万5200平方メートルに及んだ。」); <a href=\"https://www.hirosakipark.jp/outline.html\"><code>hirosakipark-outline</code></a> - 「a park whose total area reaches about 492,000 square meters (about 149,000 tsubo)」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「総面積約49万2000平方メートル（約14万9000坪）にも及ぶ公園です」) (5 by 6 ken is roughly 9 by 11 m, about 99 square meters; the ridge height is 16 m on the park's page)",
 "fn-193": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E5%A7%AB%E8%B7%AF%E8%97%A9\"><code>himeji-han-jawiki</code></a> - 「The assessed yield was 520,000 koku at first, later 150,000 koku.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「石高ははじめ52万石、のち15万石。」) (Ikeda Terumasa's enfeoffment of 1600; the domain was later far smaller)",
 "fn-194": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E5%9F%8E%E4%B8%8B%E7%94%BA\"><code>jokamachi-jawiki</code></a> - 「Terrain such as rivers was skillfully used, and moats were dug, earthen ramparts and stone walls built」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「川など地形を巧みに利用するとともに、堀を掘り、土塁や石垣を築き」) (the article's own default is the castle at the center; that a flank position goes with a river or the sea is this page's reading of the cases listed)",
 "fn-195": "<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roving_bridge\"><code>roving-bridge-enwiki</code></a> - 「Bridges were also necessary at canal junctions and where the towpath was interrupted by side arms.」 (British canal practice, the one tradition read on the point)",
 "fn-196": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E5%B7%9D%E8%B6%8A%E5%9F%8E\"><code>kawagoe-castle-jawiki</code></a> - 「The castellan's residence was in the ninomaru until it burned down, and the ninomaru also held the armaments office.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「城主の住居は焼失するまで二の丸にあり、二の丸には武具方役所もあった。」) (the residence and a domain office stood in the ninomaru until the 1846 fire; the article does not itself say the domain's business was done there)",
 "fn-197": "<a href=\"https://zh.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E7%A6%8F%E5%BB%BA%E5%9C%9F%E6%A5%BC\"><code>fujian-tulou-zhwiki</code></a> - 「Its function is four generations under one roof, dwelling and defense combined」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「其功能是四代同堂、居防合一」) (the second clause is this page's own judgment about what a seat of government announces)",
 "fn-198": "<a href=\"https://www.nagoyajo.city.nagoya.jp/learn/construction/nawabari/\"><code>nagoyajo-nawabari</code></a> - 「The enclosures are rectangular, in straight lines, a simple composition.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「曲輪は方形で直線上と単純な構成です。」); <a href=\"https://www.nagoyajo.city.nagoya.jp/learn/construction/nawabari/\"><code>nagoyajo-nawabari</code></a> - 「Compared with the complicated layouts of Sengoku-period castles, which prized being hard to assault, it is said to be straight-lined and simple.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「戦国時代の城の攻め込みにくさを重視した複雑な縄張に比べると、直線的でシンプルだといわれます。」) (Nagoya is the case read; Osaka and Nijo are this page's reading of their plans)",
 "fn-199": "<a href=\"https://kotobank.jp/word/%E6%B2%B3%E5%B2%B8-459508\"><code>kotobank-kashi</code></a> - 「The bank of a river; in particular, the place where cargo is taken up from and down into boats」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「川の岸。特に、船から荷を上げ下ろしする所」); <a href=\"https://kotobank.jp/word/%E6%B2%B3%E5%B2%B8-459508\"><code>kotobank-kashi</code></a> - 「administratively it was a town or village」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「行政的には町・村であり」) (that it is not a route is this page's inference from what the landing was)",
 "fn-200": "<a href=\"https://kotobank.jp/word/%E8%94%B5%E5%B1%8B%E6%95%B7-58201\"><code>kotobank-kurayashiki</code></a> - 「Most of the kurayashiki were concentrated near Nakanoshima and the Tosabori and Edobori rivers, convenient for water transport」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「蔵屋敷の多くは、水運に便利な中之島や土佐堀川、江戸堀川付近に集中していた」) (the case is the daimyo's Osaka warehouse compounds, cited for their waterside siting)",
 "fn-201": "<a href=\"https://www.waterworks.metro.tokyo.lg.jp/kouhou/pr/tamagawa/rekishishiritai/rekishi\"><code>tokyo-waterworks-tamagawa-rekishi</code></a> - 「In June of the following year, distribution mains of stone conduit and wooden conduit were laid underground throughout the city of Edo」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「翌年６月には江戸市中の地下に石樋、木樋による配水管を布設し」) (the open cut and the buried mains are the waterworks' own account of one system; that the vocabulary has only these three members is this page's inference from the systems it has read)",
 "fn-202": "<a href=\"https://www.town.kanra.lg.jp/kyouiku/bunkazai/bunkazai/20200722123543.html\"><code>kanra-ashigaru-nagaya</code></a> - 「built as dwellings for low-ranking domain samurai」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「下級藩士の住まいとして作られ」); <a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E8%B6%B3%E8%BB%BD\"><code>ashigaru-jawiki</code></a> - 「Ashigaru were strictly distinguished from samurai status (shibun)」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「足軽は士分と厳しく峻別され」) (the Rokugani half rests on the setting's own budget notes, which are canon rather than evidence)",
 "fn-203": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E6%AD%A6%E5%AE%B6%E5%B1%8B%E6%95%B7\"><code>bukeyashiki-wiki</code></a> - 「Further outside the samurai residences were placed the ashigaru's residences (ashigaru-yashiki, kumi-yashiki) and row houses (ashigaru-nagaya, kumi-nagaya)」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「侍屋敷の更に外側には足軽の屋敷（足軽屋敷、組屋敷）や長屋（足軽長屋、組長屋）が置かれ」); <a href=\"https://www.town.kanra.lg.jp/kyouiku/bunkazai/bunkazai/20200722123543.html\"><code>kanra-ashigaru-nagaya</code></a> - 「A five-unit row house of two-bay plan (an estimated ten units), each unit 2 ken of frontage by 3.5 ken deep」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「2間割5軒長屋（推定で10軒）で、1軒の規模が間口2間・奥行3.5間」) (ashigaru terraces is this page's rendering of the row-house form the article names)"
};
